"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Freiburg im Breisgau, Baden-Württemberg, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2676, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Datastar vs. Alpine/HTMX — Why Datastar Is the Better Fit for Craft CMS\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:31:44','2026-11-13 15:31:44','efe2e319-7f9c-478c-b240-d906a14b92b4'),(2677,2677,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Why I prefer Datastar for Craft CMS: shared signals, DOM morphing and Twig-driven updates — with a fair look at where Sprig and Alpine still fit.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [1821]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:31:44','2026-11-13 15:31:44','d12aae89-edbe-4df1-93b7-c90875579f62'),(2678,2678,1,NULL,'__temp_bzahrhiwuwbnfrboqxprwycyvhqpwpuoxidu',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">When building dynamic Craft CMS frontends, Alpine.js and HTMX are familiar choices. It\'s a popular combo — and it works. But after using both stacks on real Craft projects, I switched to Datastar and haven\'t looked back.</p><p>Here\'s why.</p><h2>The Hypermedia Renaissance</h2><p>For years, the frontend world moved in one direction: more JavaScript, more complexity, more frameworks. React, Vue, Angular — and with them, build pipelines, hydration strategies, client-side state management, and a growing gap between backend and frontend.</p><p>Then came a quiet counter-movement. HTMX, Hotwire, and others reminded developers that the web was built on hypermedia — and that server-rendered HTML, sent over the wire, is still a powerful and underrated approach.</p><p>Datastar takes this further. It\'s not just \\\"less JavaScript\\\" — it\'s a complete rethink of where state lives, how the DOM updates, and what the browser is actually good at. The server drives the UI. The browser renders it. No SPA overhead, no hydration tax, no JavaScript fatigue.</p><p>For Craft CMS developers, this is a natural fit. Craft has always been server-first, Twig-first, content-first. Datastar speaks the same language.</p><h2>Where Sprig and Alpine Need More Coordination</h2><p>Sprig brings HTMX into Craft as a first-class plugin — and it works well for isolated, self-contained components. A search box here, a filtered list there. Simple, declarative, Twig-friendly.</p><p>Sprig uses HTMX to re-render Twig components and update selected parts of the page. That is a practical model. When a page also has shared filters, live counters and browser-side interactions, I have found that coordinating Sprig and Alpine can require more decisions:</p><ul><li><strong>State ownership</strong> — decide which values belong in form fields, URL parameters, Alpine stores or the server.</li><li><strong>DOM boundaries</strong> — replacement can affect local state and JavaScript widgets. Targeted swaps, preservation and morphing extensions help, but need deliberate setup.</li><li><strong>Component communication</strong> — Sprig supports refreshing other components and triggering events; the refresh relationships still need to be designed.</li><li><strong>Shareable URLs</strong> — Sprig supports push and replace URL behavior. Filters must still be restored from those URLs, just as they must with Datastar.</li><li><strong>Multiple updates</strong> — out-of-band swaps are useful. For my projects, a shared signal model and morphing responses are often easier to coordinate.</li></ul><p>These are architectural trade-offs, not things Sprig cannot do. My preference for Datastar comes from reducing the amount of coordination I need to maintain.</p><hr /><h2>A Unified Model Changes Everything</h2><p>Datastar is not just another library — it\'s a unified model. One tool that handles both server communication and client-side reactivity, built around the same server-first philosophy that makes Craft great.</p><p>For many interactions, I can use one set of attributes instead of coordinating two libraries. Third-party widgets still need careful initialization and cleanup.</p><p>Signals hold reactive values in the browser and can be patched by the server. Craft remains responsible for authoritative data, validation and permissions. Craft renders Twig; Datastar patches the response into the page.</p><hr /><h2>Why Datastar Fits Craft CMS Better Than Sprig</h2><h3>1. Shared State Across the Entire Page</h3><p>Sprig has explicit component boundaries, with form values, URL parameters and refresh events connecting them. That can be a useful separation. I prefer a shared signal store when several parts of the page need the same reactive values.</p><p>Datastar provides a page-wide signal store. A filter value can be read by a results list, a counter and a reset button without a separate state bridge. I namespace signals so reusable components do not accidentally share state.</p><h3>2. DOM Morphing That Doesn\'t Break Your UI</h3><p>A replacement swap can discard local state inside its target. Sprig also offers targeted replacement and preservation, and its documentation describes using a morphing extension. It is not limited to replacing every component in full.</p><p>Datastar uses DOM morphing for element patches by default. It reconciles the returned HTML with the existing nodes, which helps preserve browser state. Stable IDs and clear ownership boundaries still matter, especially around maps, sliders and other JavaScript integrations.</p><h3>3. Polling Done Right</h3><p>Both Sprig and Datastar can poll an endpoint. In Datastar, <strong>data-on-interval</strong> can run an expression that sends a request; the server still performs its queries and renders the response. Morphing can reduce DOM disruption, but does not automatically reduce server work or response size.</p><p>Datastar also supports streamed updates over Server-Sent Events. Long-lived streams are possible with PHP, but require suitable worker capacity, timeouts and buffering settings. A short request-response interaction is often enough for a Craft website; persistent connections are an architectural choice, not a requirement.</p><h3>4. A Natural Next Step From Sprig</h3><p>If you know Sprig, parts of Datastar will feel familiar: server-rendered HTML, Twig templates and no separate frontend application. Signals and streamed responses introduce their own concepts, so I would migrate a real feature first rather than rewrite a working project wholesale.</p><p>The payoff, for me, is a more consistent model across server requests and browser-side reactivity.</p><h3>5. One Tool Instead of Two</h3><p>Sprig handles server communication. Alpine handles client reactivity. You handle everything in between.</p><p>Datastar does both — natively, consistently, without glue code.</p><hr /><h2>What Makes Datastar Fundamentally Different</h2><p>These aren\'t just feature differences — they\'re philosophical ones.</p><h3>The Server Is the Source of Truth</h3><p>I keep business rules, saved content and permissions on the server. Datastar signals handle transient browser state, such as an open panel or an unsent search term. They are untrusted input, not a substitute for server-side validation. Sprig and Alpine can follow the same principle; Datastar gives me a convenient way to implement it.</p><h3>Progressive Enhancement by Design</h3><p>Datastar can enhance standard links and forms, but a useful no-JavaScript path is not automatic. I provide real URLs, form actions and server-rendered results first, then enhance the interaction. The same care is needed with Sprig. A button whose only behavior is a Datastar expression will not work without JavaScript.</p><h3>Fat Morph — Send the Whole Page, Patch What Changed</h3><p>Datastar can reconcile a larger HTML region rather than requiring every change to have its own tiny response target. That can simplify template composition. The full returned HTML still travels over the network, so response size, query performance and stable element IDs still deserve attention.</p><h3>A CQRS-Style Pattern When You Need It</h3><p>You can separate a long-lived GET stream for updates from short-lived POST requests for mutations. This is a useful pattern for live interfaces, but collaboration still needs backend event delivery, authorization and a hosting setup that supports the streams. Datastar supplies the browser-side primitives, not that entire application architecture.</p><h3>Server-Confirmed Mutations</h3><p>For content changes, I prefer to show a loading state, wait for validation and update the UI from the server response. Datastar supports this straightforwardly. It is a design choice, not a guarantee provided by the library; errors and retries still need useful feedback.</p><hr /><h2>When Sprig Is Still a Good Choice</h2><p>Simple, isolated interactions — a toggle, a basic component reload, a single filtered list — Sprig handles perfectly. If your project never needs shared state or real-time updates, Sprig is a solid choice.</p><p>A working Sprig implementation does not need replacing just because another tool exists. Team experience, existing components and project requirements matter. I choose Datastar when its shared signals and response model make the next feature simpler.</p><hr /><h2>Useful Datastar Attributes in One Place</h2><p>Several everyday behaviors are available through one consistent attribute system. Some have equivalents in HTMX or Alpine; the advantage for me is using them together without another integration layer.</p><p><strong>data-on-intersect</strong> fires when an element enters the viewport — perfect for lazy-loaded content or scroll-triggered requests, no JavaScript needed. <strong>data-indicator</strong> automatically tracks request state for loading spinners and disabled buttons, purely in HTML. <strong>data-computed</strong> creates reactive derived values that stay in sync automatically. And debounce/throttle are built directly into the event system as modifiers — <strong>data-on:input__debounce.300ms</strong> adds a delay before an input handler runs; a live search also needs a signal binding, a request action and an endpoint.</p><p>For URL sync specifically: <strong>data-query-string</strong> (Pro) keeps signal values in sync with URL parameters including browser history — a convenient option rather than a capability exclusive to Datastar. Sprig also supports pushing and replacing URLs.</p><p>Small things. But they add up. 👉 <a href=\\\"https://data-star.dev/reference/attributes\\\">See the full list of Datastar attributes</a></p><hr /><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Sprig and Alpine are good tools. But for modern Craft CMS projects, Datastar fits the architecture better — server-first, Twig-native, with shared signals and streaming support when the project needs it.</p><p>Less glue code. Fewer bugs. More fun to build with.</p><hr /><h2>Datastar in Craft CMS — Made Easy by Ben Croker</h2><p>Getting started with Datastar in Craft is straightforward — thanks to the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\"><strong>Craft Datastar Plugin</strong></a> by Ben Croker of PutYourLightsOn.</p><p>The plugin handles the Craft-specific integration out of the box: SSE responses from Twig templates, action routing, and all the wiring that would otherwise require custom boilerplate. It\'s the missing piece that makes Datastar a first-class citizen in Craft — just like Sprig did for HTMX.</p><p>A big thank you to Ben Croker for building and maintaining this. Without it, the barrier to getting started with Datastar in Craft would be significantly higher.</p><p>👉 <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\">putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar</a></p><hr /><p>If you want to see Datastar in action inside real Craft CMS projects, take a look at the live demos at craft-kit.dev:</p><ul><li><a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\"><strong>Dynamic Blog</strong></a> — filtering and pagination without a page reload</li><li><a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\"><strong>Todo List</strong></a> — add, complete and delete tasks with server-confirmed updates</li><li><a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\"><strong>Map Filtering</strong></a> — location-based filtering with live updates</li><li><a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\"><strong>Live Search</strong></a> — instant search results, server-driven</li></ul><p>These examples show how the starter kit puts the approach into practice. Try a demo, then inspect its Twig templates and Datastar endpoints on GitHub. The <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}#matrix\\\">Builder demo</a> also shows how Matrix and CKEditor share the same component templates.</p><p>The full source code is available on GitHub — feel free to explore, fork, and use it as a starting point:<br />👉 <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit</a></p><p>For a deeper dive into the philosophy behind Datastar, read The Tao of Datastar:<br />👉 <a href=\\\"https://data-star.dev/guide/the_tao_of_datastar\\\">https://data-star.dev/guide/the_tao_of_datastar</a></p><p>For Craft-specific integration details, use the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\">Datastar plugin documentation</a>. For independent Craft examples, see <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.data-star.dev/\\\">Datastar &amp; Craft CMS Demos</a>. For the comparison above, the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/sprig\\\">Sprig documentation</a> also covers preservation, URL updates and component refreshes.</p><hr /><p><strong>Building a Craft CMS project and want to go the Datastar route?</strong> I\'m Andi Grether, a freelance Craft CMS developer specializing in exactly this stack. Happy to help — <a href=\\\"https://webworker.me\\\">webworker.me</a></p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:31:44','2026-11-13 15:31:44','55b56e65-d91d-4287-9628-ee737b36b43f'),(2679,2679,1,'Matrix and CKEditor — two editing contexts',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Two editing contexts: composing a page and writing a document.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:40:46','2026-11-13 15:40:46','3d6da9c8-ff8f-46b1-9c3f-7f680255049b'),(2680,2680,1,'Craft CMS 6 — looking inside the next generation',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"A look inside the next generation of a content platform.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:06','2026-11-13 15:41:06','c79586dd-19ef-486f-b7de-f48f028074be'),(2681,2681,1,'Choosing Craft — a durable foundation',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"A durable foundation, chosen with care.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:06','2026-11-13 15:41:06','f4053b5d-12b6-4ae0-8f42-51b49f4ddeb8'),(2682,2682,1,'Craft and WordPress — different building approaches',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Two building approaches, chosen to fit the project.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:06','2026-11-13 15:41:06','9b1ae29b-62ca-449f-ae15-cdff4403cf43'),(2683,2683,1,'Datastar and Vue — choosing the layers you need',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"A direct path, with additional layers only where they help.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:06','2026-11-13 15:41:06','d3ca6cf9-d89e-45bb-a224-3dea27e1a1bd'),(2684,2684,1,'Scaling content operations',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"An organized workflow with room to grow.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:07','2026-11-13 15:41:07','4de82eaf-1f04-44c0-9d05-e1a2ce50154b'),(2685,2685,1,'Bitcoin — an open network for value',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"An open network for transferring value.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:07','2026-11-13 15:41:07','418fe667-9f24-4ff1-874d-cc86a75d877b'),(2686,2686,1,'Craft CMS — shared craftsmanship',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Quality comes from how the pieces fit together.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:07','2026-11-13 15:41:07','fc4f297a-ba41-441c-8c0c-b12c72a7c68f'),(2687,2687,1,'Twig, Tailwind and Zed — tools in alignment',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Templates, styles and formatting in alignment.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:07','2026-11-13 15:41:07','db312e74-be66-4c5d-a451-d2da9ebc774e'),(2688,2688,1,'Datastar and Alpine with HTMX — coordinating reactive UI',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Different ways to coordinate a reactive interface.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:08','2026-11-13 15:41:08','790c318f-6f2f-4e8c-b5d3-98ae70bd7e35'),(2689,2689,1,'Craft multisite — many sites, one foundation',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Many distinct sites on one shared foundation.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:08','2026-11-13 15:41:08','1f784c71-5509-4187-ad70-8416810a2815'),(2690,2690,1,'Hypermedia — a direct path with fewer moving parts',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"A direct path with fewer moving parts.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:08','2026-11-13 15:41:08','d08fc686-cbe0-44e1-b576-eb1685bfc417'),(2691,2691,1,'AI-ready content — readable structure',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Clear structure makes content easier to read and reuse.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:09','2026-11-13 15:41:09','dc7d76c4-bbc7-4211-82f1-9cb17ffe9e57'),(2692,2692,1,'Craft and Laravel — a module in a larger ecosystem',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"A content platform joining a broader ecosystem.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:09','2026-11-13 15:41:09','27aabc34-d38a-4c2f-83be-6c1e1cb852b5'),(2693,2693,1,'Matrix vs. CKEditor in Craft CMS — When to Use Which','matrix-vs.-ckeditor-in-craft-cms-when-to-use-which','blog/matrix-vs.-ckeditor-in-craft-cms-when-to-use-which','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-03-09 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 30, \"lat\": 48.7784485, \"lng\": 9.1800132, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Stuttgart\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Baden-Württemberg\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Stuttgart, Baden-Württemberg, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2693, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Matrix vs. CKEditor in Craft CMS — When to Use Which\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','dc1961c9-f9b8-4c32-b985-cf2a1720af17'),(2694,2694,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Matrix or CKEditor? The answer depends on one thing: is your editor designing a page or writing a document. Here\'s how I decide on every project.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2679]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','e79bd6f8-fa8e-4ae6-ac44-c89bc82209ae'),(2695,2695,1,NULL,'__temp_juobbbpdiysymdqzztnjvbjkfrcoemvtcbej',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">One of the most common questions when starting a new Craft CMS project: should I build this with Matrix blocks or CKEditor components?</p><p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Both are powerful. Both are editor-friendly. But they serve fundamentally different purposes — and choosing the wrong one creates friction for editors and developers alike.</p><p>Here\'s how I make the decision on every project.</p><h2>The Short Answer</h2><p><strong>Matrix</strong> — for landing pages, flexible layouts, and structured page building. <strong>CKEditor</strong> — for long-form articles, editorial content, and document-style pages.</p><h2>When I Use Matrix</h2><p>Matrix is my go-to whenever the page itself <i>is</i> the product. Landing pages, campaign pages, homepages, service pages — content that needs to be composed visually, block by block, with precise control over layout and presentation.</p><p>The reason is simple: Matrix gives you a structured, predictable content model. Each block is a defined entry type with its own fields, its own template, and its own layout rules. You can build a proper grid system around it — full-width sections, content columns, popout elements, feature rows — and the editor always knows exactly what they\'re building.</p><p>In Craft Kit, Matrix blocks use a section wrapper for backgrounds and spacing, with one shared content container for width and alignment. The page stays predictable without a custom layout grid.</p><p>You can see this in action in the <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}#matrix\\\">Craft Kit Matrix Builder demo</a>.</p><p><strong>Use Matrix when:</strong></p><ul><li>The page is composed of distinct visual sections</li><li>Layout control matters — different blocks need different widths or spacing</li><li>Content is highly structured and unlikely to flow as prose</li><li>You need clean, maintainable templates per block type</li><li>Multiple stakeholders touch the same page</li></ul><h2>When I Use CKEditor</h2><p>CKEditor is my go-to for content that <i>reads</i>. Long articles, blog posts, documentation, case studies, news — anything where the editor is writing, not designing.</p><p>CKEditor keeps editors in a focused writing environment. The content flows as prose, and you can enrich it with structured components — cards, quotes, galleries, buttons, callouts — inserted inline without breaking the writing flow. These components are defined once as CKEditor entries and reused across the entire site.</p><p>The key advantage: editors don\'t have to think about layout. They write, they insert a component where it makes sense, and the template handles the rest. It\'s a much faster and more natural workflow for editorial teams.</p><p>You can see this in action in the <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}#ckeditor\\\">Craft Kit CKEditor Builder demo</a>.</p><p><strong>Use CKEditor when:</strong></p><ul><li>The content is primarily long-form text</li><li>Editors need a focused, distraction-free writing experience</li><li>Components need to be embedded inline within flowing content</li><li>The page structure is consistent and doesn\'t vary section by section</li><li>Content is authored frequently by non-technical editors</li></ul><h2>Can You Use Both?</h2><p>Yes — and on many projects I do.</p><p>A typical setup: the page uses Matrix for the main structural sections (hero, feature blocks, CTA), and one of those Matrix blocks contains a CKEditor field for the long-form body content. You get the layout control of Matrix with the editorial freedom of CKEditor, exactly where each one makes sense.</p><h2>One Block, Two Contexts</h2><p>What makes this approach especially powerful in practice: a well-structured block component can work in both Matrix <i>and</i> CKEditor — without duplicating template code.</p><p>The idea is straightforward. Each block template checks the context it\'s being rendered in — Matrix builder or CKEditor field — and adjusts its wrapper, layout classes, and structure accordingly. The block content itself stays identical.</p><p>When rendered as a Matrix block, the component gets a <i>&lt;section&gt;</i> wrapper and a shared content container. When rendered inside CKEditor, it gets a lightweight <i>&lt;div&gt;</i> wrapper that opts out of prose typography styles. Two completely different layout contexts — same component logic underneath.</p><p>This pattern keeps your codebase DRY, makes components reusable across page types, and means you only ever maintain one version of each block. Build it once, use it everywhere — in a landing page builder and inside a long-form article at the same time.</p><p>If you want to see exactly how this is implemented, the base templates for both contexts are available in the Craft Kit repository:</p><p>👉 <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/tree/main/templates/_partials\\\">partials on GitHub</a></p><h2>The Rule of Thumb</h2><blockquote><p><strong>If the editor is designing a page — use Matrix. If the editor is writing a document — use CKEditor.</strong></p></blockquote><p>It\'s not about which tool is more powerful. It\'s about which one fits the mental model of the person using it. Get that right, and both editors and developers will thank you.</p><p><strong>Want to see both in action?</strong> The Craft Kit demos show real implementations of both approaches — built with Craft CMS 5, fully server-rendered, and ready to explore or fork.</p><ul><li><a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}#matrix\\\">Matrix Builder Demo</a></li><li><a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}#ckeditor\\\">CKEditor Builder Demo</a></li><li><a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">GitHub</a></li></ul><hr /><p><strong>This article is based on how I structure real Craft CMS projects for agencies.</strong> I\'m Andi Grether, a freelance Craft CMS developer specializing in clean, maintainable architectures — with a focus on hypermedia-first approaches using Datastar and Twig.</p><p>If you\'re building a Craft CMS project and want a reliable technical partner, feel free to reach out — <a href=\\\"https://webworker.me\\\">webworker.me</a></p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','7c107d5e-7c6a-47eb-8350-f4922adbc302'),(2696,2696,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Matrix vs. CKEditor in Craft CMS — When to Use Which\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Matrix or CKEditor? Learn when to use each in Craft CMS projects — and how to build block components that work in both contexts without duplicating code.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2679]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','62647e7f-4cfc-459e-be70-a68dda1aa728'),(2697,2697,1,'Craft CMS 6: A Forward-Looking Deep Dive by ContentReactor','craft-cms-6-a-forward-looking-deep-dive-by-contentreactor','blog/craft-cms-6-a-forward-looking-deep-dive-by-contentreactor','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-12 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [1135], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 31, \"lat\": 48.1988955, \"lng\": 16.3497251, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Vienna\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"Neubaugasse, KG Neubau, Neubau\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Austria\", \"postcode\": \"1070\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Neubaugasse, KG Neubau, Neubau, Vienna, 1070, Austria\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2697, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"value\": \"https://contentreactor.com/en/insights/craft-6\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Craft CMS 6: A Forward-Looking Deep Dive by ContentReactor\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','5f4ab162-c1fc-430f-903b-f5120693a6fb'),(2698,2698,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>The team at ContentReactor explores what Craft CMS 6 brings to the table — from improved multisite workflows to enhanced authoring tools — and why it’s shaping up to be a major milestone for modern content platforms.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2680]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','618806b7-38c2-48df-b3b5-bbcea0eae586'),(2699,2699,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Craft CMS 6: A Forward-Looking Deep Dive by ContentReactor\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"The team at ContentReactor explores what Craft CMS 6 brings to the table — from improved multisite workflows to enhanced authoring tools — and why it’s shaping up to be a major milestone for modern content platforms.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2680]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','0c467c6e-6075-4546-9cb3-612a5ddacad9'),(2700,2700,1,'Why I Still Choose Craft CMS — Block 81','why-i-still-choose-craft-cms-block-81','blog/why-i-still-choose-craft-cms-block-81','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-12 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [1135], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 32, \"lat\": 45.5202471, \"lng\": -122.674194, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Portland\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Oregon\", \"county\": \"Multnomah County\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"United States\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Portland, Multnomah County, Oregon, United States\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2700, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"value\": \"https://block81.com/blog/why-i-still-choose-craft-cms\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Why I Still Choose Craft CMS — Block 81\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','a8a872ba-5f9f-4f70-a873-4b77e6238c54'),(2701,2701,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>A seasoned digital agency explains why Craft CMS continues to outperform other systems — from flexible content modeling to a clean authoring experience and powerful built-in tools. A quick, convincing read for anyone choosing their next CMS.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2681]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','94587de0-8826-4b07-8979-8df76ec6ed70'),(2702,2702,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Why I Still Choose Craft CMS — Block 81\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"A seasoned digital agency explains why Craft CMS continues to outperform other systems — from flexible content modeling to a clean authoring experience and powerful built-in tools. A quick, convincing read for anyone choosing their next CMS.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2681]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','446a5b10-3582-4aa2-a6d3-035841fa8933'),(2703,2703,1,'Why Craft CMS Might Be a Better Fit Than WordPress','craft-vs-wordpress','blog/craft-vs-wordpress','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-10-20 22:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 33, \"lat\": 51.5074456, \"lng\": -0.1277653, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"London\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Greater London, England\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"United Kingdom\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"London, Greater London, England, United Kingdom\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2703, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Why Craft CMS Might Be a Better Fit Than WordPress\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','90cfd842-f49f-447f-bc7c-a84c40e2a37e'),(2704,2704,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Tired of plugin chaos and theme bloat? Craft CMS might be the breath of fresh air you need.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2682]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','c401a680-af85-4bd1-bafa-95c2143c66c6'),(2705,2705,1,NULL,'__temp_epuxgsbappmctwahfdpzjemvchoaohitwwwg',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">For many agencies and developers, WordPress has been the go-to CMS for years. It’s familiar, has a massive ecosystem, and is well-known to clients.</p><p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">But <i>familiarity isn’t always the same as fit</i>.</p><p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Craft CMS isn’t trying to be WordPress — and that’s exactly why it’s winning the hearts of designers, developers, and clients who want clean, flexible sites without the plugin chaos.</p><h2><strong>Clean by Default</strong></h2><p>WordPress often starts out lean but quickly turns into a plugin maze. Craft CMS, on the other hand, comes with just what you need — nothing more, nothing less. No “Hello Dolly,” no page builder you didn’t ask for, no endless menu items.</p><p>👉 <strong>Result:</strong> Faster projects, fewer surprises, and cleaner codebases.</p><h2><strong>Developer First (But Friendly for Editors)</strong></h2><p>Craft is designed with developers in mind — structured content modeling, clean templating, and predictable behavior. But it also gives editors a <i>beautiful and intuitive</i> authoring experience. No clutter, no 30 different side panels. If you’ve ever tried to train a client in WordPress Gutenberg, you’ll know what I mean.</p><h2><strong>Performance Without a Fight</strong></h2><p>With WordPress, good performance often requires caching plugins, optimization tools, and server tweaks. Craft is fast out of the box — Twig templates, caching layers, and asset transforms are built in. That means fewer dependencies, less debugging, and a snappier experience overall.</p><h2><strong>Freedom for Designers</strong></h2><p>WordPress often bends you towards themes and blocks. Craft gives you a blank canvas. That’s why agencies love it — you can build <i>your</i> design system, not someone else’s. It’s like the difference between renting a furnished apartment and building your own loft.</p><h2><strong>Conclusion</strong></h2><p>WordPress isn’t bad — but Craft CMS is often a better fit for modern teams who want clarity, control, and clean output.</p><p>If you want a deeper breakdown of why teams move from WordPress to Craft — especially around performance, security, and content flexibility — the official comparison is worth a look:<br />👉 <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/vs-wordpress\\\">Craft CMS vs WordPress</a></p><p>If you’re thinking about switching, Craft even provides a clear migration path from WordPress.  <br />👉 <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/blog/migrating-from-wordpress\\\">Official guide: Migrating from WordPress</a></p><p>✅ <strong>Tip:</strong> If you’re setting up Craft CMS for the first time, try starting from my a well-structured <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">starter kit</a>. It saves hours of setup time.</p><p> </p><p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\"> </p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','23bd4c57-09d4-4f1f-be51-6ad27f942e63'),(2706,2706,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2682]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','59b7d8a1-5533-4b02-9909-2877449a1daa'),(2707,2707,1,'Why I Prefer Datastar Over Vue + GraphQL for Most Craft CMS Projects','why-i-prefer-datastar-over-vue-and-graphql','blog/why-i-prefer-datastar-over-vue-and-graphql','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-01-14 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 34, \"lat\": 48.8534951, \"lng\": 2.3483915, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Paris\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Ile-de-France\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"France\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Paris, Ile-de-France, Metropolitan France, France\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2707, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Why I Prefer Datastar Over Vue + GraphQL for Most Craft CMS Projects\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','7ff466d8-9809-4ff5-b092-85c7279aaa93'),(2708,2708,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Datastar gives me the speed and simplicity I need for most projects — without the overhead of a full frontend framework. Here’s why I often skip Vue + GraphQL.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2683]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','4de56dca-7208-45ad-a01b-5a11b7498679'),(2709,2709,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Why I Prefer Datastar Over Vue + GraphQL for Most Craft CMS Projects\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Datastar gives me the speed and simplicity I need for most projects — without the overhead of a full frontend framework. Here’s why I often skip Vue + GraphQL.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2683]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','496a73a8-c095-4d82-9fb6-418d860c3994'),(2710,2710,1,'How Craft CMS Helps Businesses Scale Their Content Operations by Appfoster','how-craft-cms-helps-businesses-scale-their-content-operations-by-appfoster','blog/how-craft-cms-helps-businesses-scale-their-content-operations-by-appfoster','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-12 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [1135], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 12, \"lat\": 43.8349486, \"lng\": -79.5074666, \"zoom\": 9, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Vaughan\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Golden Horseshoe, Ontario\", \"county\": \"York Region\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"9131\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"Keele Street\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Canada\", \"postcode\": \"L4K 5B4\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"9131, Keele Street, Vaughan, York Region, Golden Horseshoe, Ontario, L4K 5B4, Canada\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2710, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"value\": \"https://appfoster.com/web-development/how-craft-cms-help-businesses-scale-their-content-operations\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"How Craft CMS Helps Businesses Scale Their Content Operations by Appfoster\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','e3a3f857-0d76-4f6f-bac2-dc0ff51b6f51'),(2711,2711,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Appfoster presents a compelling case for how Craft CMS enables organizations to grow their content workflows efficiently — leveraging structured content models, powerful integrations and intuitive authoring experiences to support enterprise-scale operations.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2684]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','ce1989f1-df03-4886-af2c-3ebad44828f4'),(2712,2712,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"How Craft CMS Helps Businesses Scale Their Content Operations by Appfoster\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Appfoster presents a compelling case for how Craft CMS enables organizations to grow their content workflows efficiently — leveraging structured content models, powerful integrations and intuitive authoring experiences to support enterprise-scale operations!\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2684]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:26','2026-11-13 15:41:26','f4e75484-c259-4b07-b236-670c4208c9cc'),(2713,2713,1,'Bitcoin: The Open Protocol for Value','bitcoin-the-open-protocol-for-value','blog/bitcoin-the-open-protocol-for-value','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-06 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 13, \"lat\": 50.1106444, \"lng\": 8.6820917, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Frankfurt\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Hesse\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Frankfurt, Hesse, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2713, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"⚡ Bitcoin: The Open Protocol for Value\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','87742440-407e-4d99-adfb-0a8bd0500aa9'),(2714,2714,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Bitcoin isn’t just “crypto.” It’s an open protocol for transferring value — global, secure, and unstoppable. A system built on code, not trust.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2685]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','223bbfe8-a329-4929-9fab-c52cb8318ac9'),(2715,2715,1,NULL,'__temp_xywfexnhyxzniuroeiuvyfhpbiwlqqqfblwo',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Bitcoin is no longer just a speculative asset — it’s an <strong>open, decentralized protocol</strong>, much like the Internet itself. Except instead of moving data, it moves <strong>value</strong> — instantly, globally, and without permission.</p><h2><strong>🧠 Why It Matters</strong></h2><p>Today’s financial system runs on trust in institutions. Bitcoin replaces that trust with <strong>math and code</strong>. The rules are transparent, and no one can change them — not banks, not governments, not developers.</p><p><strong>21 million Bitcoin.</strong></p><p>No more, no less. Ever.</p><h2><strong>🔋 Energy, Security &amp; Misconceptions</strong></h2><p>Yes — Bitcoin uses energy.</p><p>But that energy <strong>secures the network</strong>, making it the most resilient computing system on the planet. It has been running for over <strong>15 years without interruption</strong> — no hacks, no bailouts, no downtime.</p><h2><strong>🧩 The Real Innovation</strong></h2><p>Bitcoin isn’t “crypto.” It’s the opposite. It’s simple, minimal, and stable by design. No founders, no marketing department — just open-source code and a global network of users keeping it alive.</p><p>It’s a <strong>protocol</strong>, not a product.</p><h2><strong>🏁 Final Thought</strong></h2><p>Whether you see it as a store of value, digital cash, or an idea — Bitcoin is about <strong>freedom, transparency, and self-sovereignty</strong>.</p><p>It’s not about getting rich. It’s about <strong>getting free</strong>.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','c2a3a3e1-430b-413a-b046-64debcd00c4b'),(2716,2716,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Bitcoin — The Open Protocol for Value\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Bitcoin is more than digital money — it’s an open, decentralized protocol for transferring value without banks or borders. Built on math, code, and freedom.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2685]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','5ed88344-2979-42a8-aa99-4c98623b957f'),(2717,2717,1,'Craft CMS – Built for teams that care about quality','craft-cms-built-for-teams-that-care-about-quality','blog/craft-cms-built-for-teams-that-care-about-quality','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-09 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 14, \"lat\": 40.416782, \"lng\": -3.703507, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Madrid\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Community of Madrid\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Spain\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Madrid, Community of Madrid, Spain\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2717, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Craft CMS —\\nBuilt for teams that care about quality\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','1c5e28f9-ec6f-40e5-bd45-28a76dc55fe4'),(2718,2718,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Craft CMS gives creative teams full control — no limits, no plugin chaos, no compromise.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2686]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','5fc0f613-5258-40d7-ba75-fe4c22b80af5'),(2719,2719,1,NULL,'__temp_whefxcmmzovbxnoomtjpsrynwzlivarixbef',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Behind every great website is a tool that empowers, not restricts.</p><p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Craft CMS is built for creators who take their work seriously — developers who love clean, flexible code, designers who value creative freedom, and marketing teams who need speed and control.</p><p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">It’s not just a CMS — it’s the foundation for digital experiences that last.</p><h2>🚀 Why Craft CMS stands out</h2><p><strong>Craft CMS isn’t another theme-based builder.</strong></p><p>It’s a professional content platform that puts flexibility and creative control first — so your team can build faster, scale smarter, and design without compromise.</p><ul><li>✨ Modern, secure, and future-proof</li><li>⚙️ Custom structures instead of rigid templates</li><li>🧩 Clean architecture and Twig templating</li><li>🌍 Multilingual and SEO-ready</li><li>💻 Built for both editors and developers</li></ul><p>Craft gives your team a reliable foundation to create websites, portals, and web apps that perform — and age gracefully.</p><h2>💼 For marketing &amp; content teams</h2><p>Craft CMS is made for marketers who want freedom without friction. The authoring experience is clean, intuitive, and perfectly suited for fast-moving campaigns.</p><ul><li>🖋️ Create and update content in seconds</li><li>🧱 Build landing pages using flexible content blocks</li><li>🌍 Manage multilingual campaigns with ease</li><li>🔄 Clear approval workflows and role management</li><li>📈 Optimize SEO and analytics without external tools</li></ul><p>No cluttered dashboards, no technical barriers — just a CMS that lets you move at the speed of ideas.</p><h2>💻 For developers &amp; agencies</h2><p>Developers love Craft because it’s built with them in mind. It’s logical, modular, and plays nicely with modern frameworks and workflows.</p><ul><li>🧠 Clean, modern codebase (PHP, Twig, Yii2 → Laravel from v6)</li><li>🔗 REST &amp; GraphQL APIs for headless or hybrid setups</li><li>⚡ Datastar for reactive UIs without heavy JavaScript frameworks</li><li>🧩 Extendable through plugins, modules &amp; CLI tools</li><li>🧱 Works seamlessly with Tailwind, Vue, Alpine.js &amp; Vite</li><li>🔒 Fully versionable, CI/CD-ready, and docker-compatible</li></ul><p>With <strong>Craft + DataStar + Laravel</strong>, developers get the best of both worlds — a powerful CMS engine combined with a robust PHP framework and a modern, reactive front-end layer.</p><p>From small brand sites to complex web platforms, Craft delivers flexibility, speed, and long-term maintainability.</p><h2>🎨 For designers &amp; creatives</h2><p>Design without compromise. Craft gives designers full freedom to translate creative concepts directly into functional, responsive layouts.</p><ul><li>🎨 No theme limitations</li><li>🧭 Full control over markup &amp; components</li><li>📱 Responsive, accessible, and pixel-perfect</li><li>💡 Ideal for design systems and modular layouts</li></ul><p>It’s design-first, without the chaos.</p><h2>🌍 A growing community</h2><p>Craft CMS is continuously improved, open-code, and supported by a vibrant global community. More than 50,000 websites worldwide rely on it — from independent studios to established brands.</p><p>It’s the platform behind digital craftsmanship: reliable, flexible, and built to last.</p><h2>🧩 Summary</h2><blockquote><p>Craft CMS isn’t a shortcut.<br />It’s a tool for people who build things the right way — with purpose, precision, and pride.</p></blockquote><p>Clean, fast, flexible — and designed for teams that care about what they create.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','4858b347-e3a8-4454-ba01-940d3da6115c'),(2720,2720,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Craft CMS – The modern CMS for creative teams and serious developers\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Craft CMS gives creative teams the freedom to build without limits. Designed for marketers, developers, and brands that value performance, design, and control.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2686]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','b6ea3c2f-634a-4327-afdc-e5dca1813252'),(2721,2721,1,'Tailwind + Twig in the Zed Editor','tailwind-twig-and-the-zed-editor','blog/tailwind-twig-and-the-zed-editor','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-17 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 19, \"lat\": 53.550341, \"lng\": 10.000654, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Hamburg\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Hamburg, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2721, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Tailwind + Twig in the Zed Editor\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','5c3f5775-f9da-4bee-9677-f6d6d0c94cf9'),(2722,2722,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Making Tailwind IntelliSense, Twig syntax &amp; Prettier all work together</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2687]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','5e04ebb3-5e1b-45cb-9b1d-0c5a79657bf8'),(2723,2723,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Tailwind + Twig in the Zed Editor\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Making Tailwind IntelliSense, Twig syntax & Prettier all work together\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2687]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:41:27','2026-11-13 15:41:27','a495f878-bfbf-4366-a789-68ca3c697684'),(2724,2724,1,'Datastar vs. Alpine/HTMX — Why Datastar Is the Better Fit for Craft CMS','datastar-vs-alpine-h