: \"Neubaugasse, KG Neubau, Neubau\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Austria\", \"postcode\": \"1070\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Neubaugasse, KG Neubau, Neubau, Vienna, 1070, Austria\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2769, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"value\": \"https://contentreactor.com/en/insights/craft-6\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Craft CMS 6: A Forward-Looking Deep Dive by ContentReactor\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','36622234-f59e-43f7-8a2c-825cf4e43c1c'),(2770,2770,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Craft CMS 6: A Forward-Looking Deep Dive by ContentReactor\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"ContentReactor explores the direction of Craft CMS 6, from its Laravel foundation to editorial tools. A useful look ahead for teams planning Craft projects.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2680]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','c44d0652-2701-4938-a7d9-bfd21df586c8'),(2771,2771,1,'Why I Still Choose Craft CMS — Block 81','why-i-still-choose-craft-cms-block-81','blog/why-i-still-choose-craft-cms-block-81','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-12 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [1135], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 32, \"lat\": 45.5202471, \"lng\": -122.674194, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Portland\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Oregon\", \"county\": \"Multnomah County\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"United States\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Portland, Multnomah County, Oregon, United States\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2771, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"value\": \"https://block81.com/blog/why-i-still-choose-craft-cms\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Why I Still Choose Craft CMS — Block 81\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','bcf21e73-a663-49d5-973a-4c2d1815d59a'),(2772,2772,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Why I Still Choose Craft CMS — Block 81\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Block 81 explains why Craft CMS remains its choice for client work: flexible content models, a focused editing experience and control over the frontend.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2681]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','90eccbb0-a2b2-4586-8660-ef1a7e451820'),(2773,2773,1,'Why Craft CMS Might Be a Better Fit Than WordPress','craft-vs-wordpress','blog/craft-vs-wordpress','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-10-20 22:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 33, \"lat\": 51.5074456, \"lng\": -0.1277653, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"London\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Greater London, England\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"United Kingdom\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"London, Greater London, England, United Kingdom\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2773, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Why Craft CMS Might Be a Better Fit Than WordPress\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','bc471c64-90e4-468d-919e-3164c7686e80'),(2774,2774,1,NULL,'__temp_epuxgsbappmctwahfdpzjemvchoaohitwwwg',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">For a custom, content-driven website, I want control over the content model, the markup and the way the interface behaves. Craft CMS gives me that foundation. Datastar makes it reactive without turning it into a separate frontend application.</p><h2>A content model built around the project</h2><p>Craft starts with sections, entry types, fields and relationships that you define. Editors work with the structure their content needs, rather than a theme\'s assumptions. Matrix handles page sections; CKEditor handles flowing text with embedded components.</p><p>WordPress can also support custom content models and carefully built frontends. My preference is about the starting point: Craft makes structured content and custom Twig templates central to the workflow.</p><h2>Own the HTML</h2><p>With Twig, the same project controls content queries, semantic markup, image output and accessible controls. There is no requirement to choose a theme or expose a JSON API before a page can exist.</p><p>That is a useful foundation for performance, but not a guarantee. Queries, eager loading, caching, image sizes and hosting still need attention.</p><h2>Add Datastar where the page needs to react</h2><p>A filter does not need a second application. Craft can render the results in Twig, and Datastar can request and morph those results into the page. Search, pagination and a task list follow the same pattern.</p><p>In Craft Kit, Datastar also controls the navigation dropdowns, mobile navigation and search panel. There is no Alpine, Vue or React layer coordinating that ordinary UI state. The same signal and attribute model covers local interactions and server requests.</p><h2>Choose the stack for the work</h2><p>WordPress remains a valid choice for teams invested in its ecosystem. Craft is the stack I choose when a project calls for a deliberate content architecture, a custom design and a frontend I can keep understandable.</p><p>The official <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/vs-wordpress\\\">Craft CMS and WordPress comparison</a> covers the differences in more detail. For an existing WordPress site, start with the <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/blog/migrating-from-wordpress\\\">migration guide</a> and audit the content before planning a rebuild.</p><h2>Try the approach, not just the argument</h2><p>Explore the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">Datastar blog</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map filters</a> and <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">live search</a>. Then inspect the <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}\\\">Builder</a> to see how the content is composed.</p><p><a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Download Craft Kit on GitHub</a> and use the templates as a starting point for your own Craft and Datastar project.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','c632f057-b3c7-416f-ba49-5867ae7cfb30'),(2775,2775,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Craft gives you control over content models and Twig output. See why it is my starting point for custom websites, with Datastar adding the reactive layer.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2682]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','62c8c448-d44e-43b6-a95d-cabb4f77ac4e'),(2776,2776,1,'Why I Prefer Datastar Over Vue + GraphQL for Most Craft CMS Projects','why-i-prefer-datastar-over-vue-and-graphql','blog/why-i-prefer-datastar-over-vue-and-graphql','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-01-14 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 34, \"lat\": 48.8534951, \"lng\": 2.3483915, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Paris\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Ile-de-France\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"France\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Paris, Ile-de-France, Metropolitan France, France\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2776, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Why I Prefer Datastar Over Vue + GraphQL for Most Craft CMS Projects\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','49b2db37-b226-4ecf-9110-91911e4ab691'),(2777,2777,1,NULL,'__temp_jstciagbxuaohtngrbfunmncynfgajzhhnep',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">I used to reach for Craft, GraphQL and Vue whenever a website needed interactivity. For most content-driven projects, I now prefer Craft, Twig and Datastar. The reason is practical: I can deliver the same useful interactions with fewer layers to coordinate.</p><h2>What Vue and GraphQL solve</h2><p>A separate frontend application makes sense when it is genuinely an application: complex offline behavior, substantial browser-owned data, or several clients consuming the same API. Craft\'s GraphQL API can serve a web frontend, a mobile app and other integrations from one content model.</p><p>That is a different requirement from a website that needs search, filters and pagination. I do not want to introduce an API boundary solely to update a list of Craft entries.</p><h2>What the extra layer costs</h2><p>Craft generates a GraphQL schema from the content model and permissions; you do not have to invent that schema manually. You still need to maintain frontend queries, rendering components, loading states and error handling around the API.</p><p>For a single website, that can mean expressing the same content relationships in Craft, in API queries and in the frontend\'s rendering layer. Those boundaries can be valuable for an independent frontend team. In a small Craft team, I often find they add coordination without enough benefit.</p><h2>Let Craft render the response</h2><p>Craft already knows how to query entries, resolve relations, transform assets and render Twig. Datastar lets those templates remain the UI: an interaction requests an endpoint, the server returns HTML patches, and the browser updates the relevant elements.</p><p>The response can finish immediately. An SSE response does not require a permanent connection, and a live-search interaction does not require a dedicated streaming server.</p><p>Datastar signals handle transient browser state. Craft owns saved data, permissions and validation. The boundary is explicit without a separate frontend store or client-side router.</p><h2>One model for everyday interactions</h2><p>This site is a working example. The mobile menu, navigation dropdowns and search panel use Datastar, just like the blog filters, map filters and Todo List. There is no Alpine layer for the small interactions and another framework for the bigger ones.</p><p>Specialist tools still do specialist work: Leaflet draws the map, Embla handles the slider and GLightbox opens galleries. Datastar coordinates the ordinary UI and server-driven updates; it does not need to replace a mapping engine.</p><h2>When I would still choose Vue and GraphQL</h2><p>I would choose them when the product needs a separately deployed application, an API shared across several clients, or complex browser-side workflows that justify that architecture. This is not a claim that a server-rendered website can replace every application.</p><h2>See the smaller stack in practice</h2><p>Try the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">filterable blog</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">live search</a> and <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a>. Each demonstrates a useful interaction while Craft remains responsible for the response.</p><p>For most Craft websites, that is the default I want: content in Craft, markup in Twig, reactivity in Datastar. <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Craft Kit gives you the working starting point.</a></p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','5bb17c9e-bc3e-48aa-ad55-bd13820e5c6d'),(2778,2778,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Why I Prefer Datastar Over Vue + GraphQL for Most Craft CMS Projects\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"For content-driven Craft sites, I prefer Twig and Datastar to a separate Vue application. Less coordination, server-rendered updates and one place to build.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2683]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','9067e90d-1171-4397-be1c-254840a854e9'),(2779,2779,1,'How Craft CMS Helps Businesses Scale Their Content Operations by Appfoster','how-craft-cms-helps-businesses-scale-their-content-operations-by-appfoster','blog/how-craft-cms-helps-businesses-scale-their-content-operations-by-appfoster','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-12 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [1135], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 12, \"lat\": 43.8349486, \"lng\": -79.5074666, \"zoom\": 9, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Vaughan\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Golden Horseshoe, Ontario\", \"county\": \"York Region\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"9131\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"Keele Street\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Canada\", \"postcode\": \"L4K 5B4\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"9131, Keele Street, Vaughan, York Region, Golden Horseshoe, Ontario, L4K 5B4, Canada\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2779, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"value\": \"https://appfoster.com/web-development/how-craft-cms-help-businesses-scale-their-content-operations\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"How Craft CMS Helps Businesses Scale Their Content Operations by Appfoster\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','d3145453-0c3a-4472-ba75-312dc4658b1a'),(2780,2780,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"How Craft CMS Helps Businesses Scale Their Content Operations by Appfoster\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Appfoster looks at scaling content operations with Craft CMS: structured models, reusable content and editorial workflows that can grow with the business.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2684]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','3b791f67-ebd4-4c9b-ba71-701a09c80e54'),(2781,2781,1,'Bitcoin: The Open Protocol for Value','bitcoin-the-open-protocol-for-value','blog/bitcoin-the-open-protocol-for-value','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-06 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 13, \"lat\": 50.1106444, \"lng\": 8.6820917, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Frankfurt\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Hesse\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Frankfurt, Hesse, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2781, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"⚡ Bitcoin: The Open Protocol for Value\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','3101cd1b-8330-4321-9a57-d5086e8c63d2'),(2782,2782,1,NULL,'__temp_xywfexnhyxzniuroeiuvyfhpbiwlqqqfblwo',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Bitcoin is an open protocol for transferring value. What interests me is the architecture: public rules, independently operated nodes and a network that does not depend on one application\'s database.</p><h2>Shared rules, independently checked</h2><p>Participants can run software that verifies transactions and blocks against the protocol\'s rules. Control is distributed across a network rather than assigned to a single bank or platform.</p><p>The current consensus rules limit issuance to approximately 21 million bitcoin. Those rules are maintained through software and network agreement, not an administrator changing a balance in a central system.</p><h2>Security has costs and limits</h2><p>Proof of work consumes energy to make rewriting transaction history expensive. That does not remove every risk: wallets can be compromised, services can fail and transactions require care. Confirmation times and fees also vary.</p><p>Bitcoin is neither an instant-payment guarantee nor a promise of financial returns. This article is a technical introduction, not investment advice.</p><h2>Why there is a Bitcoin panel in Craft Kit</h2><p>The small Bitcoin button in this site\'s navigation is also a Datastar example. Open it and Craft fetches the BTC/USDT ticker from Binance on the server, renders the result in Twig and sends an HTML update to the browser.</p><p>While the panel is open, Datastar runs the refresh countdown and requests an update roughly every ten seconds. When it is closed, the interval does not request ticker updates. The open state, countdown and request indicator all use Datastar signals and attributes.</p><p>The visitor\'s browser talks to this website, not directly to Binance. Market data is illustrative and may be delayed or unavailable; it is not an execution price.</p><h2>The useful takeaway</h2><p>A live data panel does not need a separate frontend application. The same server-rendered pattern powers the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog filters</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">search</a> and <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a>.</p><p><a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Explore the Craft Kit source on GitHub</a> to see the Datastar attributes and Twig endpoints behind the panel.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','49922334-6dc1-4c28-8964-aa67d1404b5b'),(2783,2783,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Bitcoin — The Open Protocol for Value\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Bitcoin is an open protocol for transferring value. Explore the idea of shared rules and a decentralized network, then try the Datastar-powered price demo.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2685]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','5843d4e4-154d-499e-865a-a20c436a300b'),(2784,2784,1,'Craft CMS – Built for teams that care about quality','craft-cms-built-for-teams-that-care-about-quality','blog/craft-cms-built-for-teams-that-care-about-quality','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-09 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 14, \"lat\": 40.416782, \"lng\": -3.703507, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Madrid\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Community of Madrid\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Spain\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Madrid, Community of Madrid, Spain\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2784, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Craft CMS —\\nBuilt for teams that care about quality\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','0c57252e-e086-4866-877c-63e39f08d2c4'),(2785,2785,1,NULL,'__temp_whefxcmmzovbxnoomtjpsrynwzlivarixbef',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">A good content platform should give editors a clear workflow, designers control over the result and developers a codebase they can still understand after handoff. That is why I build with Craft CMS, Twig and Datastar.</p><h2>Start with the content</h2><p>Craft lets you model the actual work: pages, articles, locations and reusable components, with fields and relationships that match the project. Editors see the inputs they need, rather than a generic page builder with every option switched on.</p><p>In Craft Kit, Matrix composes page sections and CKEditor handles text with embedded components. Both use the same Twig component templates. Explore the <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}\\\">Builder demo</a> to see the distinction.</p><h2>Keep the interface close to the templates</h2><p>Datastar adds reactivity without replacing Craft\'s rendering model. Twig still produces the markup. A filter change requests new results; an update returns HTML; signals keep the browser\'s transient state coordinated.</p><p>This is not reserved for the headline demos. Navigation dropdowns, the mobile menu and the search panel use Datastar too. There is no Alpine, React or Vue layer for everyday interface state.</p><h2>A shared foundation for the team</h2><ul><li><strong>Editors</strong> work with structured, reusable content rather than raw layout instructions.</li><li><strong>Designers</strong> get custom markup and styling instead of a theme\'s constraints.</li><li><strong>Developers</strong> can follow a feature from its HTML attributes to the Twig endpoint that renders the response.</li></ul><p>Craft Kit includes Tailwind and Vite for styling and asset builds. Specialist libraries handle the map, slider and gallery; Datastar owns the ordinary reactive UI. The stack is deliberate, not dependency-free.</p><h2>Quality is implemented, not automatic</h2><p>Accessibility, sensible query limits, eager loading, cache boundaries and server-side validation still need to be built. A smaller rendering architecture makes those decisions easier to see; it does not make them disappear.</p><h2>Try it before choosing it</h2><p>Use the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog filters</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">interactive map</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">search</a> and <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a>. Then look at how the templates implement them.</p><p><a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Download Craft Kit on GitHub</a>. It is a starter kit for your own Craft and Datastar work, not a finished client website or a mandatory design system.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','4517c61a-e896-4797-a106-2f64bbd735ff'),(2786,2786,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Craft CMS – The modern CMS for creative teams and serious developers\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"A clear content model, maintainable Twig and reactive UI with Datastar. See how Craft Kit brings editors, designers and developers onto a shared foundation.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2686]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:18','2026-11-13 15:53:18','36a6b334-45be-4ff7-bcd4-5a474d78d6d4'),(2787,2787,1,'Tailwind + Twig in the Zed Editor','tailwind-twig-and-the-zed-editor','blog/tailwind-twig-and-the-zed-editor','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-11-17 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 19, \"lat\": 53.550341, \"lng\": 10.000654, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Hamburg\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Hamburg, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2787, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Tailwind + Twig in the Zed Editor\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','5cbcabb9-c4a6-42fe-b21a-ea2f2cbb3ccd'),(2788,2788,1,NULL,'__temp_vzhwwxlrcexubrqwmorpeiowbdqyozhwoacz',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">A focused editor setup makes Twig and Datastar work easier to follow. In Zed, start with Twig syntax support, then verify Tailwind completion and formatting against the versions in your project.</p><h2>Twig support</h2><p>Install a maintained Twig extension and read its current setup instructions. The <a href=\\\"https://github.com/YussufSassi/zed-twig\\\">Zed Twig extension README</a> describes its language-server and Emmet integration. Extension behavior can change, so test a real template rather than copying settings from an older screenshot.</p><p>Datastar attributes remain ordinary HTML attributes in those templates. You do not need a separate component language or an Alpine setup to work on Craft Kit\'s reactive UI.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','a2890ea6-236e-4e7a-85fc-dfee97b9120b'),(2789,2789,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','bcef880f-e51b-4074-93e7-841dc75821ce'),(2790,2790,1,NULL,'__temp_qowybonnqcvzwobvxqfaucbcbwessmwuxurg',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<h2>Formatting and completion</h2><p>Use the project\'s existing Prettier configuration and Twig plugin. Add Tailwind completion using the editor\'s current language-server setup, making sure it recognizes Twig files and the CSS entry point used by Tailwind v4.</p><p>Do not install a second formatter or class-sorting plugin just because a guide lists it. First check the project dependencies and confirm that saving a template produces the expected diff.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','82c00481-8fee-4eeb-ba29-6c8655a22939'),(2791,2791,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','e42d2eff-3ab9-4a66-8455-fe0af160020b'),(2792,2792,1,NULL,'__temp_btrakhakhltdejmlxuspasfgovtunhqhugcp',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<h2>Verify the workflow</h2><ul><li>Open a real Twig component and check syntax highlighting.</li><li>Check class completion against the project\'s actual Tailwind setup.</li><li>Format the file and review the diff.</li><li>Run the project build and inspect the rendered result.</li></ul><p>The goal is a reliable editing workflow, not a large extension collection. Explore the <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}\\\">Builder</a> and the <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Craft Kit templates on GitHub</a> for practical files to work with.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','9021014b-4791-42cc-92bf-5f601f98b1e7'),(2793,2793,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','a4d05938-92b6-4155-91ba-b70194ace04c'),(2794,2794,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Tailwind + Twig in the Zed Editor\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Set up Twig syntax, Tailwind completion and consistent formatting in Zed. A focused editing workflow for the templates and styles behind your Craft project.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2687]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','9c12c458-4149-4646-8506-c046b3e735c7'),(2795,2795,1,'Datastar vs. Alpine/HTMX — Why Datastar Is the Better Fit for Craft CMS','datastar-vs-alpine-htmx-why-datastar-is-the-better-fit-for-craft-cms','blog/datastar-vs-alpine-htmx-why-datastar-is-the-better-fit-for-craft-cms','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-03-08 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 25, \"lat\": 47.9960901, \"lng\": 7.8494005, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Freiburg im Breisgau\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Baden-Württemberg\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Freiburg im Breisgau, Baden-Württemberg, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2795, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Datastar vs. Alpine/HTMX — Why Datastar Is the Better Fit for Craft CMS\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','9a70083c-9644-40b4-9544-6f535831e768'),(2796,2796,1,NULL,'__temp_bzahrhiwuwbnfrboqxprwycyvhqpwpuoxidu',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">When building dynamic Craft CMS frontends, Alpine.js and HTMX are familiar choices. It\'s a popular combo — and it works. But after using both stacks on real Craft projects, I switched to Datastar and haven\'t looked back.</p><p>Here\'s why.</p><p><strong>This site is the working example.</strong> Its navigation dropdowns, mobile menu and search panel use Datastar, just like the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog filters</a> and <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a>. There is no Alpine layer for everyday UI state. Try the demos, then <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">inspect the Twig templates on GitHub</a>.</p><h2>The Hypermedia Renaissance</h2><p>For years, the frontend world moved in one direction: more JavaScript, more complexity, more frameworks. React, Vue, Angular — and with them, build pipelines, hydration strategies, client-side state management, and a growing gap between backend and frontend.</p><p>Then came a quiet counter-movement. HTMX, Hotwire, and others reminded developers that the web was built on hypermedia — and that server-rendered HTML, sent over the wire, is still a powerful and underrated approach.</p><p>Datastar takes this further. It\'s not just \\\"less JavaScript\\\" — it\'s a complete rethink of where state lives, how the DOM updates, and what the browser is actually good at. The server drives the UI. The browser renders it. No SPA overhead, no hydration tax, no JavaScript fatigue.</p><p>For Craft CMS developers, this is a natural fit. Craft has always been server-first, Twig-first, content-first. Datastar speaks the same language.</p><h2>Where Sprig and Alpine Need More Coordination</h2><p>Sprig brings HTMX into Craft as a first-class plugin — and it works well for isolated, self-contained components. A search box here, a filtered list there. Simple, declarative, Twig-friendly.</p><p>Sprig uses HTMX to re-render Twig components and update selected parts of the page. That is a practical model. When a page also has shared filters, live counters and browser-side interactions, I have found that coordinating Sprig and Alpine can require more decisions:</p><ul><li><strong>State ownership</strong> — decide which values belong in form fields, URL parameters, Alpine stores or the server.</li><li><strong>DOM boundaries</strong> — replacement can affect local state and JavaScript widgets. Targeted swaps, preservation and morphing extensions help, but need deliberate setup.</li><li><strong>Component communication</strong> — Sprig supports refreshing other components and triggering events; the refresh relationships still need to be designed.</li><li><strong>Shareable URLs</strong> — Sprig supports push and replace URL behavior. Filters must still be restored from those URLs, just as they must with Datastar.</li><li><strong>Multiple updates</strong> — out-of-band swaps are useful. For my projects, a shared signal model and morphing responses are often easier to coordinate.</li></ul><p>These are architectural trade-offs, not things Sprig cannot do. My preference for Datastar comes from reducing the amount of coordination I need to maintain.</p><hr /><h2>A Unified Model Changes Everything</h2><p>Datastar is not just another library — it\'s a unified model. One tool that handles both server communication and client-side reactivity, built around the same server-first philosophy that makes Craft great.</p><p>For many interactions, I can use one set of attributes instead of coordinating two libraries. Third-party widgets still need careful initialization and cleanup.</p><p>Signals hold reactive values in the browser and can be patched by the server. Craft remains responsible for authoritative data, validation and permissions. Craft renders Twig; Datastar patches the response into the page.</p><hr /><h2>Why Datastar Fits Craft CMS Better Than Sprig</h2><h3>1. Shared State Across the Entire Page</h3><p>Sprig has explicit component boundaries, with form values, URL parameters and refresh events connecting them. That can be a useful separation. I prefer a shared signal store when several parts of the page need the same reactive values.</p><p>Datastar provides a page-wide signal store. A filter value can be read by a results list, a counter and a reset button without a separate state bridge. I namespace signals so reusable components do not accidentally share state.</p><h3>2. DOM Morphing That Doesn\'t Break Your UI</h3><p>A replacement swap can discard local state inside its target. Sprig also offers targeted replacement and preservation, and its documentation describes using a morphing extension. It is not limited to replacing every component in full.</p><p>Datastar uses DOM morphing for element patches by default. It reconciles the returned HTML with the existing nodes, which helps preserve browser state. Stable IDs and clear ownership boundaries still matter, especially around maps, sliders and other JavaScript integrations.</p><h3>3. Polling Done Right</h3><p>Both Sprig and Datastar can poll an endpoint. In Datastar, <strong>data-on-interval</strong> can run an expression that sends a request; the server still performs its queries and renders the response. Morphing can reduce DOM disruption, but does not automatically reduce server work or response size.</p><p>Datastar also supports streamed updates over Server-Sent Events. Long-lived streams are possible with PHP, but require suitable worker capacity, timeouts and buffering settings. A short request-response interaction is often enough for a Craft website; persistent connections are an architectural choice, not a requirement.</p><h3>4. A Natural Next Step From Sprig</h3><p>If you know Sprig, parts of Datastar will feel familiar: server-rendered HTML, Twig templates and no separate frontend application. Signals and streamed responses introduce their own concepts, so I would migrate a real feature first rather than rewrite a working project wholesale.</p><p>The payoff, for me, is a more consistent model across server requests and browser-side reactivity.</p><h3>5. One Tool Instead of Two</h3><p>Sprig handles server communication. Alpine handles client reactivity. You handle everything in between.</p><p>Datastar does both — natively, consistently, without glue code.</p><hr /><h2>What Makes Datastar Fundamentally Different</h2><p>These aren\'t just feature differences — they\'re philosophical ones.</p><h3>The Server Is the Source of Truth</h3><p>I keep business rules, saved content and permissions on the server. Datastar signals handle transient browser state, such as an open panel or an unsent search term. They are untrusted input, not a substitute for server-side validation. Sprig and Alpine can follow the same principle; Datastar gives me a convenient way to implement it.</p><h3>Progressive Enhancement by Design</h3><p>Datastar can enhance standard links and forms, but a useful no-JavaScript path is not automatic. I provide real URLs, form actions and server-rendered results first, then enhance the interaction. The same care is needed with Sprig. A button whose only behavior is a Datastar expression will not work without JavaScript.</p><h3>Fat Morph — Send the Whole Page, Patch What Changed</h3><p>Datastar can reconcile a larger HTML region rather than requiring every change to have its own tiny response target. That can simplify template composition. The full returned HTML still travels over the network, so response size, query performance and stable element IDs still deserve attention.</p><h3>A CQRS-Style Pattern When You Need It</h3><p>You can separate a long-lived GET stream for updates from short-lived POST requests for mutations. This is a useful pattern for live interfaces, but collaboration still needs backend event delivery, authorization and a hosting setup that supports the streams. Datastar supplies the browser-side primitives, not that entire application architecture.</p><h3>Server-Confirmed Mutations</h3><p>For content changes, I prefer to show a loading state, wait for validation and update the UI from the server response. Datastar supports this straightforwardly. It is a design choice, not a guarantee provided by the library; errors and retries still need useful feedback.</p><hr /><h2>When Sprig Is Still a Good Choice</h2><p>Simple, isolated interactions — a toggle, a basic component reload, a single filtered list — Sprig handles perfectly. If your project never needs shared state or real-time updates, Sprig is a solid choice.</p><p>A working Sprig implementation does not need replacing just because another tool exists. Team experience, existing components and project requirements matter. I choose Datastar when its shared signals and response model make the next feature simpler.</p><hr /><h2>Useful Datastar Attributes in One Place</h2><p>Several everyday behaviors are available through one consistent attribute system. Some have equivalents in HTMX or Alpine; the advantage for me is using them together without another integration layer.</p><p><strong>data-on-intersect</strong> fires when an element enters the viewport — perfect for lazy-loaded content or scroll-triggered requests, no JavaScript needed. <strong>data-indicator</strong> automatically tracks request state for loading spinners and disabled buttons, purely in HTML. <strong>data-computed</strong> creates reactive derived values that stay in sync automatically. And debounce/throttle are built directly into the event system as modifiers — <strong>data-on:input__debounce.300ms</strong> adds a delay before an input handler runs; a live search also needs a signal binding, a request action and an endpoint.</p><p>For URL sync specifically: <strong>data-query-string</strong> (Pro) keeps signal values in sync with URL parameters including browser history — a convenient option rather than a capability exclusive to Datastar. Sprig also supports pushing and replacing URLs.</p><p>Small things. But they add up. 👉 <a href=\\\"https://data-star.dev/reference/attributes\\\">See the full list of Datastar attributes</a></p><hr /><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Sprig and Alpine are good tools. But for modern Craft CMS projects, Datastar fits the architecture better — server-first, Twig-native, with shared signals and streaming support when the project needs it.</p><p>Less glue code. Fewer bugs. More fun to build with.</p><hr /><h2>Datastar in Craft CMS — Made Easy by Ben Croker</h2><p>Getting started with Datastar in Craft is straightforward — thanks to the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\"><strong>Craft Datastar Plugin</strong></a> by Ben Croker of PutYourLightsOn.</p><p>The plugin handles the Craft-specific integration out of the box: SSE responses from Twig templates, action routing, and all the wiring that would otherwise require custom boilerplate. It\'s the missing piece that makes Datastar a first-class citizen in Craft — just like Sprig did for HTMX.</p><p>A big thank you to Ben Croker for building and maintaining this. Without it, the barrier to getting started with Datastar in Craft would be significantly higher.</p><p>👉 <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\">putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar</a></p><hr /><p>If you want to see Datastar in action inside real Craft CMS projects, take a look at the live demos at craft-kit.dev:</p><ul><li><a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\"><strong>Dynamic Blog</strong></a> — filtering and pagination without a page reload</li><li><a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\"><strong>Todo List</strong></a> — add, complete and delete tasks with server-confirmed updates</li><li><a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\"><strong>Map Filtering</strong></a> — location-based filtering with live updates</li><li><a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\"><strong>Live Search</strong></a> — instant search results, server-driven</li></ul><p>These examples show how the starter kit puts the approach into practice. Try a demo, then inspect its Twig templates and Datastar endpoints on GitHub. The <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}#matrix\\\">Builder demo</a> also shows how Matrix and CKEditor share the same component templates.</p><p>The full source code is available on GitHub — feel free to explore, fork, and use it as a starting point:<br />👉 <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit</a></p><p>For a deeper dive into the philosophy behind Datastar, read The Tao of Datastar:<br />👉 <a href=\\\"https://data-star.dev/guide/the_tao_of_datastar\\\">https://data-star.dev/guide/the_tao_of_datastar</a></p><p>For Craft-specific integration details, use the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\">Datastar plugin documentation</a>. For independent Craft examples, see <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.data-star.dev/\\\">Datastar &amp; Craft CMS Demos</a>. For the comparison above, the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/sprig\\\">Sprig documentation</a> also covers preservation, URL updates and component refreshes.</p><hr /><p><strong>Building a Craft CMS project and want to go the Datastar route?</strong> I\'m Andi Grether, a freelance Craft CMS developer specializing in exactly this stack. Happy to help — <a href=\\\"https://webworker.me\\\">webworker.me</a></p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','a27a2d5b-8ddf-4b6c-a5a2-163fa7b5eb5a'),(2797,2797,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Datastar vs. Alpine & HTMX – The Better Choice for Craft CMS\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Why I choose Datastar for Craft CMS: shared signals, DOM morphing and Twig-driven updates, with a practical comparison to Sprig, HTMX and Alpine.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2688]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','30f7a73b-3e47-4fbb-a67c-c81e2dc1efd6'),(2798,2798,1,'How Craft CMS Powers High-Scalability Multi-Site Setups (70+ Sites) — Fredmansky','how-craft-cms-powers-high-scalability-multi-site-setups-70-sites-fredmansky','blog/how-craft-cms-powers-high-scalability-multi-site-setups-70-sites-fredmansky','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2025-12-02 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [1135], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 36, \"lat\": 48.3142913, \"lng\": 14.2813602, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Linz\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Upper Austria\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"58\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"Hauptstraße, Alt-Urfahr, Urfahr\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Austria\", \"postcode\": \"4020\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"58, Hauptstraße, Alt-Urfahr, Urfahr, Linz, Upper Austria, 4020, Austria\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2798, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"96a98d05-9c97-4424-b5b3-b60a09dafe54\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"label\": \"Read more\", \"value\": \"https://fredmansky.at/a/craft-cms-meets-high-scalability\"}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"How Craft CMS Powers High-Scalability Multi-Site Setups (70+ Sites) — Fredmansky\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','ae425ee7-2f81-4f5a-bb9c-61de2a60496a'),(2799,2799,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Fredmansky shares how one Craft installation supports more than 70 sites. A German-language case study in shared architecture and multisite content workflows.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2689]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','692dcb03-1667-4a45-ab83-3f0546220035'),(2800,2800,1,'Craft CMS & Hypermedia: How Agencies Build Less and Deliver More','craft-cms-hypermedia-how-agencies-build-less-and-deliver-more','blog/craft-cms-hypermedia-how-agencies-build-less-and-deliver-more','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-01-22 23:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [876], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 41, \"lat\": 50.1106444, \"lng\": 8.6820917, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Frankfurt\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"Hesse\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Frankfurt, Hesse, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2800, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Craft CMS & Hypermedia: How Agencies Build Less and Deliver More\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','7b1c293c-c7d9-4ed2-870e-8197d775786a'),(2801,2801,1,NULL,'__temp_pvyunrzlevypxishthzotzqimbsgpuwcomzq',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">For an agency, every extra layer becomes something to explain, deploy and maintain. Craft CMS and Datastar offer a useful alternative for content-driven websites: keep the content model, Twig templates and interactive responses in one project.</p><h2>Build the website, not a second application</h2><p>A headless CMS and a separate frontend can be the right architecture. They are not prerequisites for a search box, a filtered list or a task form.</p><p>Craft already queries the content and renders HTML. Datastar lets that HTML remain the interface while adding responsive interactions. There is no need to expose an ordinary page\'s state through a JSON API just to redraw it in another rendering system.</p><h2>One reactive model across the site</h2><p>Craft Kit uses Datastar for navigation dropdowns, the mobile menu and search-panel visibility, as well as for server requests in the blog, map, live search and Todo List. There is no Alpine layer to bridge into a second UI framework.</p><p>That consistency is the point. A developer can read an interaction\'s attributes, follow its request and find the Twig response. The small interactions use the same concepts as the larger demos.</p><h2>What gets simpler for the agency</h2><ul><li><strong>Feature work:</strong> queries and the markup they produce stay together.</li><li><strong>Handoffs:</strong> a Craft developer can trace the response without first learning a separate application.</li><li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> fewer rendering boundaries need coordinating when a content model changes.</li><li><strong>Design:</strong> semantic HTML and a project\'s own component templates remain under the team\'s control.</li></ul><p>These are reasons to expect less coordination, not guaranteed cost savings. A real project still needs testing, accessible controls, useful loading states and clear server-side permissions.</p><h2>Use the right tool at the right boundary</h2><p>Datastar owns transient UI signals and requests. Craft owns authoritative data and validation. Specialized libraries can still draw a map or move a slider; they do not need to own the page\'s content state.</p><p>A hypermedia approach also benefits from real links and forms. Where an interaction depends on JavaScript, say so and provide an appropriate alternative. Progressive enhancement is a design decision, not a label that a framework adds for you.</p><h2>Start with working examples</h2><p>Explore the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">search</a> and <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a>. The <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}\\\">Builder</a> shows how the same project models reusable editorial components.</p><p>Craft Kit is available on <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">GitHub</a>. Use it to evaluate Datastar with real Craft templates, then keep only the examples and patterns your client project needs.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','787341d2-3ce1-4c60-b465-db66762a6476'),(2802,2802,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Craft CMS & Hypermedia: How Agencies Build Less and Deliver More\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Keep the content model, templates and UI updates close together. How agencies use Craft and Datastar to reduce moving parts without giving up interactivity.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2690]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','1671fec7-b09b-470c-bd14-8c7a51afe7fa'),(2803,2803,1,'Making Craft CMS AI-Ready — What GEO Actually Means in Practice','making-craft-cms-ai-ready-what-geo-actually-means-in-practice','blog/making-craft-cms-ai-ready-what-geo-actually-means-in-practice','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-04-09 22:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 53, \"lat\": 51.2254018, \"lng\": 6.7763137, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Dusseldorf\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"North Rhine-Westphalia\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Dusseldorf, North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2803, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Making Craft CMS AI-Ready — What GEO Actually Means in Practice\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','8e09bc57-8d8e-4813-8cd6-9036b080b94c'),(2804,2804,1,NULL,'__temp_wtragnbaiqmezzfuowubqopgdsfnzyxnmjgb',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Making a website useful to AI tools starts with the same work that helps people: clear content, meaningful headings and reliable links. Craft Kit adds machine-friendly output without maintaining a second copy of every article.</p><h2>Readable content comes first</h2><p>AI tools can process HTML, but a page may contain navigation, scripts and other material that obscures its main content. A well-structured Markdown representation can make that content easier to extract and work with.</p><p>Craft Kit uses the LLM Ready plugin to offer Markdown output. Clients that request <strong>Accept: text/markdown</strong> can receive a readable representation at the same page URL. Support for that request pattern varies between tools; it is not a promise that every crawler will use it.</p><h2>One content model, several representations</h2><p>The article remains editor-managed in Craft. Twig produces the website; machine-readable output is generated from the same source. There is no parallel documentation tree for editors to keep synchronized.</p><p>This matches the reason I use Datastar for the frontend: let Craft remain responsible for the content, and return the representation the interaction needs. HTML powers the visitor\'s interface; Markdown can serve a reading tool.</p><h2>What llms.txt can and cannot do</h2><p>An auto-generated <strong>llms.txt</strong> can act as a concise index for tools that choose to read it. It is not a replacement for a sitemap, robots directives, accessible HTML or good internal linking.</p><p>There is no ranking guarantee attached to publishing it. Adoption and crawler behavior change, so I would not build a marketing promise around it. I keep the index generated rather than asking editors to maintain another manual file.</p><h2>Reuse the descriptions you already write</h2><p>A useful SEO description can also summarize an article for a content index. Reusing it avoids another field that repeats the same editorial job. The important part is an accurate summary, not filling a special AI keyword slot.</p><h2>A practical checklist</h2><ol><li>Publish useful content with a clear heading structure.</li><li>Keep canonical URLs and internal links reliable.</li><li>Provide readable Markdown for clients that request it.</li><li>Generate an index from the existing content model.</li><li>Review the actual output instead of assuming a plugin guarantees visibility.</li></ol><p>For the reactive side of the same server-first approach, try the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">Datastar blog</a> and <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">live search</a>. Their content remains rendered by Craft rather than being hidden behind a separate frontend application.</p><p><a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Explore Craft Kit on GitHub</a> to inspect the templates and configuration. Treat the AI-friendly output as a practical capability, not a guarantee of traffic, citations or rankings.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','b22f1659-312a-4b8b-9af7-7f0f2dccca2f'),(2805,2805,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Making Craft CMS AI-Ready — What GEO Actually Means in Practice\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Make Craft content easier for AI tools to read with Markdown, clear structure and reusable SEO descriptions. A practical setup, without ranking promises.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2691]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','0bbde7ad-5ace-48ca-8653-d3379c1f040f'),(2806,2806,1,'Craft CMS is Becoming a Laravel Package','craft-cms-6-laravel','blog/craft-cms-6-laravel','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-04-27 22:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [268], \"7f45d57a-6089-4476-9875-732dfa47a1a0\": {\"id\": 54, \"lat\": 52.5173885, \"lng\": 13.3951309, \"zoom\": 15, \"parts\": {\"arm\": \"Orion Arm\", \"city\": \"Berlin\", \"group\": \"the Local Group\", \"state\": \"\", \"county\": \"\", \"galaxy\": \"Milky Way\", \"number\": \"\", \"planet\": \"Earth\", \"system\": \"the Solar System\", \"address\": \"\", \"cluster\": \"Virgo Cluster\", \"country\": \"Germany\", \"postcode\": \"\", \"supercluster\": \"Laniakea Supercluster\"}, \"address\": \"Berlin, Germany\", \"fieldId\": 35, \"ownerId\": 2806, \"distance\": null, \"what3words\": null, \"ownerSiteId\": 1}, \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Craft CMS is Becoming a Laravel Package. Here is What That Means for You.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','f7b3d717-5ccc-4494-aede-ebf9597f0211'),(2807,2807,1,NULL,'__temp_dyzuagdpzcywlcsglsgmpeqdpnpfrabvemcr',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Pixel &amp; Tonic announced that Craft CMS 6 will move from Yii 2 to Laravel. For Craft developers, the interesting question is what that foundation changes — and what should remain familiar.</p><p>This article summarizes the announced direction, not the current release status. Craft Kit currently runs on Craft CMS 5. Check the <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/blog/laravel\\\">official Laravel announcement</a> and <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/knowledge-base/supported-versions\\\">supported versions</a> before planning a production upgrade.</p><h2>Why the framework matters</h2><p>The underlying framework supplies infrastructure for requests, services, commands and integrations. Laravel has an established ecosystem that Craft can build on. That creates opportunities, but does not mean every Laravel package or hosting tool automatically works with every Craft project.</p><p>Pixel &amp; Tonic described the transition as mostly a port rather than a wholesale rewrite of Craft\'s architecture. The stated aim is to keep extending the CMS familiar while changing the foundation underneath it.</p><h2>What to evaluate</h2><p>The useful questions are concrete: how custom modules boot, which APIs change, which plugin versions support the release, and what the chosen hosting environment requires. Test those against your own project rather than treating a framework name as a migration plan.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','7d76da0c-c705-4cd7-80dc-e362c4e2c4cd'),(2808,2808,1,NULL,'__temp_sopumlbdlehymcbywkjddpeesvdxwbpmyyfk',NULL,'{\"d04bc466-1b82-4e88-ba5a-d772666f5e29\": \"What the framework change means\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','7be66ccd-d12d-44d5-ab89-c8d9fa995248'),(2809,2809,1,NULL,'__temp_xbvlvqleuhqvydemtghsrjrebykqhzaowjoi',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"⚙️\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Framework foundation\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Laravel becomes the underlying framework in the announced Craft 6 direction. Evaluate framework-level changes against the actual release documentation.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','8efb9679-117c-44a8-a669-1b95dad1cbf5'),(2810,2810,1,NULL,'__temp_kaimyratqfzqrmtbxhxenksinmpblxjxuymk',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"🗃️\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Plugin compatibility\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"The announced Yii 2 adapter aims to reduce migration work. Plugin support and custom-module compatibility still need project-specific testing.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','467303d8-25c4-40fc-95ef-1d28c30a9137'),(2811,2811,1,NULL,'__temp_bbkovqgbxkjoobgnlbxannpponulgjkudqod',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"📦\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"A broader ecosystem\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Laravel offers a large package ecosystem. Choose integrations for a real requirement and verify compatibility rather than assuming every package is a drop-in.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','d20077e0-42ff-481d-a34f-53be74e76e56'),(2812,2812,1,NULL,'__temp_zikohidxtyytfoxulbdnscurbazqhjrzmvau',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"🚀\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Familiar content architecture\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"The announcement emphasizes a port of the existing architecture. Structured content and deliberate template boundaries remain useful investments.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','1ba1c9d7-89a6-4d59-8784-fb9763c457a8'),(2813,2813,1,NULL,'__temp_dvircpqzyvmogztggiuadssoggqpyqhvhwfg',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"📊\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"A tested upgrade path\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Rehearse upgrades on a local copy, check Project Config and migrations, and verify queues, assets and custom code before touching production.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','bb724402-c145-4223-87aa-f0d00926c066'),(2814,2814,1,NULL,'__temp_vnivgvksezxshwprmuwpolueoshfceqnoopx',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"🛠️\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Current release guidance\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Use official release notes and support dates for planning. A roadmap describes an intention; it is not confirmation that a feature has shipped.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','bd784606-c036-4fa2-a30e-7560379596e2'),(2815,2815,1,NULL,'__temp_daocwrgiaptvxcfgnjafqyvxvvooqagobrms',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<h2>What happens to existing projects</h2><p>The announcement includes a Yii 2 adapter intended to reduce compatibility work for Craft 5 plugins and modules. That is an aid to migration, not a guarantee that every custom integration will run unchanged. Check support from each plugin author and test the actual upgrade.</p><p>Craft 5 is an LTS release. Pixel &amp; Tonic announced support for five years after Craft 6\'s full release; use the <a href=\\\"https://craftcms.com/knowledge-base/supported-versions\\\">official support policy</a> for current dates rather than calculating them from an old roadmap.</p><h2>What editors can look forward to</h2><p>Pixel &amp; Tonic also announced editorial features for Craft 6. Treat the list below as announced plans; check the release notes for what is available in a particular version.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','e12ca30a-8edd-4f07-bf3f-e37ef2373806'),(2816,2816,1,NULL,'__temp_lqwrfrtjyioxrehlxfhluumivnrvpkgqfrcw',NULL,'{\"d04bc466-1b82-4e88-ba5a-d772666f5e29\": \"Announced editorial features\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','9eeb320a-2624-4f81-8ab4-11e1d19e02d2'),(2817,2817,1,NULL,'__temp_yypbfzsagpbfklflctpkpchweyezdiuowapm',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"✅\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Approval workflows\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Announced governance features would let teams define how content moves through review and publication. Verify availability in the version you choose.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','5ae9eaf1-5d59-4ad6-975b-96fab673067b'),(2818,2818,1,NULL,'__temp_fevpxsvfnavmrzqqfuklzbelfudcsxtmrqsx',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"💬\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Edit-page comments\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Feedback directly on an entry edit page is part of the announced editorial direction, keeping discussion close to the content it concerns.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','45ca316a-b803-4471-9c6c-5cdf5575404c'),(2819,2819,1,NULL,'__temp_biwjmbvbgpgialmddcccczhnqnjjmrediczs',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"📋\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Element activity logs\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"The announced activity timeline is intended to make changes to an element easier to follow, including who changed what and when.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','c616baaa-35d7-4534-99b0-0f9762dc06c2'),(2820,2820,1,NULL,'__temp_ypuyxwhfugyjxcjizashheeojuvutjwnnfue',NULL,'{\"3e5af703-8dda-4b8d-90ec-c328be3aa431\": \"📅\", \"487cc30b-61c6-421d-b453-84857b9cae46\": \"Content releases\", \"a88d2904-f0c9-447c-87ac-4dbd0d25d7d7\": \"Publishing related entries and drafts together, manually or on a schedule, is one of the announced improvements for coordinated launches.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','0aa943d9-c672-42c3-990f-4af233414957'),(2821,2821,1,NULL,'__temp_uflbgcwhxputwgsgexaegqeotkzofmghxvrb',NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<h2>Roadmaps are not release guarantees</h2><p>The original announcement targeted a 2026 beta and full release. Schedules and implementation details can change. Confirm the current release, migration guidance and plugin compatibility directly with Craft before setting a client deadline.</p><h2>What this means for Craft Kit and Datastar</h2><p>The architectural reason for Datastar does not depend on Laravel: Craft renders HTML, and Datastar coordinates reactive browser state and server-driven updates. A backend framework change is not a reason to introduce a separate frontend application.</p><p>Craft Kit demonstrates that approach today on Craft 5. Its navigation dropdowns, search panel, blog filters and Todo List use Datastar rather than Alpine or another general-purpose frontend framework.</p><p>Try the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog</a>, <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">search</a> and <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a>, or <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">inspect the starter kit on GitHub</a>. Build a clean content model and well-defined template boundaries now; assess any future framework migration against the actual released APIs.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','82380d28-ae5e-4a42-af89-e1c831054695'),(2822,2822,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Craft CMS 6 and Laravel — What Craft Developers Need to Know\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Craft CMS is moving towards Laravel. Learn what the announced direction means for your projects, which details need checking and why clean Twig still matters.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2692]}',1,'2026-11-13 15:53:19','2026-11-13 15:53:19','d5c56ae1-61a8-4ca8-b8f1-858cc28d454f'),(2823,2823,1,'Privacy policy','privacy-policy','privacy-policy','{\"1a9a9160-4064-4d41-b55f-e820ab14f99c\": 0, \"872f39e9-c6e7-4db6-9915-33944177d829\": \"Privacy policy\", \"981facbf-3e2f-4e4b-9f37-d1b8629dd72b\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Craft Kit is a public demonstration of a Craft CMS starter kit and its Datastar examples. It also presents my work as an independent developer. This notice explains how personal data is processed when you visit or use the demos.</p><h2>Controller and contact</h2><p>Andi Grether<br />Badstr. 22c<br />79379 Müllheim<br />Germany<br /><a href=\\\"mailto:webworker.ag@gmail.com\\\">webworker.ag@gmail.com</a></p><h2>Website delivery and server logs</h2><p>When you request a page or an asset, the hosting system processes technical information such as your IP address, request URL, time of access, browser information and response status. These data are needed to deliver the website, diagnose problems and protect it from abuse.</p><p>Logs are retained only as long as needed for these purposes or to investigate security incidents, subject to applicable retention obligations. The legal basis for operating and securing the website is our legitimate interest under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR.</p><h2>Datastar interactions, search and the Todo List</h2><p>Datastar is served from this website. Navigation controls, search, filters and the demo forms send requests to this website when a server response is needed. Search terms and submitted demo values are processed to provide the requested result. They can also form part of the requested URL and technical access logs.</p><p>The Todo List stores tasks in your server-side session. A necessary session cookie can associate your browser with that session; security cookies may also be used for request protection. The demo does not create a permanent personal task account. Do not enter sensitive or confidential information into it.</p><p>Essential session and security storage is used to provide the function you request, not for advertising. The applicable basis is Article 6(1)(f) GDPR and, for strictly necessary device storage or access, Section 25(2) TDDDG.</p><h2>Cookieless Matomo analytics</h2><p>The public website uses a self-hosted Matomo installation at <strong>p-e8qn5a.project.space</strong> to understand use of the demo. The tracker is configured with cookies disabled. It records page views and link interactions; the demos also send event categories such as adding or completing a task and opening the Bitcoin data panel. The task text is not included in those custom event calls.</p><p>Analytics can process the visited URL, referrer, time of access, browser and device information, and the IP address received by the analytics server. Cookieless tracking is still data processing. IP masking and retention are controlled in the Matomo server configuration; disabling cookies alone is not a guarantee of anonymity.</p><p>The purpose is to improve the demonstration and understand which examples are useful, based on our legitimate interest under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR. You may object to processing based on legitimate interests by contacting the controller. If a particular analytics configuration requires consent, it must be assessed separately; the absence of cookies does not decide that question by itself.</p><h2>OpenStreetMap on the map demo</h2><p>Opening the <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map demo</a> loads map tiles directly from OpenStreetMap tile servers. These requests transmit technical connection data, including your IP address and the requested tile URLs, to the map service. This is separate from the locally served Leaflet code used to display the map.</p><p>The map is provided to demonstrate location filtering. Details about the service\'s data processing are available in the <a href=\\\"https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy\\\">OpenStreetMap Foundation privacy policy</a>. Other pages do not need to load those map tiles.</p><h2>Images, fonts and frontend assets</h2><p>The demo images, fonts and frontend scripts are served from this website. The editorial illustrations were created for Craft Kit and are marked with an AI badge. Viewing them does not send an image-generation request or visitor data to an AI image service. Unsplash images and remote font services are not used.</p><h2>Server-side data sources and external links</h2><p>The GitHub repository statistics and Binance BTC/USDT ticker are fetched by the website\'s server. The demo does not send your search terms or task text to those services, and your browser does not need to contact their APIs to display the results.</p><p>Links to GitHub, LinkedIn, documentation and external articles take you to other websites when you follow them. Their operators then process data under their own privacy policies.</p><h2>Contact by email</h2><p>If you send an email, your message and contact details are processed to answer your inquiry. This includes processing by the email service providers involved in delivery. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(b) GDPR for contract-related inquiries, or Article 6(1)(f) GDPR for other correspondence. Messages are deleted when no longer needed, unless legal retention obligations apply.</p><h2>Your rights</h2><p>Subject to the applicable conditions, you may request access, rectification, erasure, restriction of processing or data portability. You may object to processing based on legitimate interests. Where processing relies on consent, you may withdraw it for the future.</p><p>You may also complain to a data protection supervisory authority. For Baden-Württemberg, see the <a href=\\\"https://www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de/\\\">state data protection authority</a>. Contact the controller above to exercise your rights.</p><h2>Reusing the starter kit</h2><p>This notice describes this demonstration, not every project built from the repository. If you reuse Craft Kit, adapt the notice to your controller details, hosting, enabled integrations and actual data-processing configuration.</p><p>Last updated: 13 November 2026.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:54:27','2026-11-13 15:54:27','35e30e30-d41f-4a10-a557-fec19f8b222e'),(2824,2824,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"How the Craft Kit demo processes technical data, Datastar demo inputs, cookieless Matomo analytics and OpenStreetMap requests.\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:54:27','2026-11-13 15:54:27','a4a43a41-32f6-44eb-b44b-446342d56f90'),(2825,2825,1,'Imprint','imprint','imprint','{\"1a9a9160-4064-4d41-b55f-e820ab14f99c\": 0, \"872f39e9-c6e7-4db6-9915-33944177d829\": \"Imprint\", \"981facbf-3e2f-4e4b-9f37-d1b8629dd72b\": \"<p>Information pursuant to § 5 DDG</p><p>Andi Grether<br />Badstr. 22c<br />79379 Müllheim<br />Germany<br /><a href=\\\"mailto:webworker.ag@gmail.com\\\">webworker.ag@gmail.com</a></p><p><strong>Legal Notice</strong></p><p>The content of this website has been created and reviewed with great care. However, no guarantee can be given for the accuracy, completeness, or current validity of the information provided. This website demonstrates the Craft Kit starter kit and presents independent development work. The demo does not provide an online checkout. </p><p><strong>Illustrations and screenshots</strong></p><p>The editorial illustrations were created for Craft Kit with an image-generation model and are marked with an AI badge. The editor screenshots show the Craft Kit demo installation. Images are served locally; no Unsplash images are used.</p><p>Last updated: 13 November 2026</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:54:27','2026-11-13 15:54:27','4dff7fe1-5ac6-4850-b0e3-7816caed0af6'),(2826,2826,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false}',1,'2026-11-13 15:54:27','2026-11-13 15:54:27','a15337e6-baae-4dd0-8010-6ba69ce1e0c9'),(2827,2827,1,'Privacy policy','privacy-policy','privacy-policy','{\"1a9a9160-4064-4d41-b55f-e820ab14f99c\": 0, \"872f39e9-c6e7-4db6-9915-33944177d829\": \"Privacy policy\", \"981facbf-3e2f-4e4b-9f37-d1b8629dd72b\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Craft Kit is a public demonstration of a Craft CMS starter kit and its Datastar examples. It also presents my work as an independent developer. This notice explains how personal data is processed when you visit or use the demos.</p><h2>Controller and contact</h2><p>Andi Grether<br />Badstr. 22c<br />79379 Müllheim<br />Germany<br /><a href=\\\"mailto:webworker.ag@gmail.com\\\">webworker.ag@gmail.com</a></p><h2>Website delivery and server logs</h2><p>When you request a page or an asset, the hosting system processes technical information such as your IP address, request URL, time of access, browser information and response status. These data are needed to deliver the website, diagnose problems and protect it from abuse.</p><p>Logs are retained only as long as needed for these purposes or to investigate security incidents, subject to applicable retention obligations. The legal basis for operating and securing the website is our legitimate interest under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR.</p><h2>Datastar interactions, search and the Todo List</h2><p>Datastar is served from this website. Navigation controls, search, filters and the demo forms send requests to this website when a server response is needed. Search terms and submitted demo values are processed to provide the requested result. They can also form part of the requested URL and technical access logs.</p><p>The Todo List stores tasks in your server-side session. A necessary session cookie can associate your browser with that session; security cookies may also be used for request protection. The demo does not create a permanent personal task account. Do not enter sensitive or confidential information into it.</p><p>Essential session and security storage is used to provide the function you request, not for advertising. The applicable basis is Article 6(1)(f) GDPR and, for strictly necessary device storage or access, Section 25(2) TDDDG.</p><h2>Cookieless Matomo analytics</h2><p>The public website uses a self-hosted Matomo installation at <strong>p-e8qn5a.project.space</strong> to understand use of the demo. The tracker is configured with cookies disabled. It records page views and link interactions; the demos also send event categories such as adding or completing a task and opening the Bitcoin data panel. The task text is not included in those custom event calls.</p><p>Analytics can process the visited URL, referrer, time of access, browser and device information, and the IP address received by the analytics server. Cookieless tracking is still data processing. IP masking and retention are controlled in the Matomo server configuration; disabling cookies alone is not a guarantee of anonymity.</p><p>The purpose is to improve the demonstration and understand which examples are useful, based on our legitimate interest under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR. You may object to processing based on legitimate interests by contacting the controller. If a particular analytics configuration requires consent, it must be assessed separately; the absence of cookies does not decide that question by itself.</p><h2>OpenStreetMap on the map demo</h2><p>Opening the <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map demo</a> loads map tiles directly from OpenStreetMap tile servers. These requests transmit technical connection data, including your IP address and the requested tile URLs, to the map service. This is separate from the locally served Leaflet code used to display the map.</p><p>The map is provided to demonstrate location filtering. Details about the service\'s data processing are available in the <a href=\\\"https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy\\\">OpenStreetMap Foundation privacy policy</a>. Other pages do not need to load those map tiles.</p><h2>Images, fonts and frontend assets</h2><p>The demo images, fonts and application UI scripts are served from this website. The Matomo analytics script is loaded separately from the analytics host described above. The editorial illustrations were created for Craft Kit and are marked with an AI badge. Viewing them does not send an image-generation request or visitor data to an AI image service. Unsplash images and remote font services are not used.</p><h2>Server-side data sources and external links</h2><p>The GitHub repository statistics and Binance BTC/USDT ticker are fetched by the website\'s server. The demo does not send your search terms or task text to those services, and your browser does not need to contact their APIs to display the results.</p><p>Links to GitHub, LinkedIn, documentation and external articles take you to other websites when you follow them. Their operators then process data under their own privacy policies.</p><h2>Contact by email</h2><p>If you send an email, your message and contact details are processed to answer your inquiry. This includes processing by the email service providers involved in delivery. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(b) GDPR for contract-related inquiries, or Article 6(1)(f) GDPR for other correspondence. Messages are deleted when no longer needed, unless legal retention obligations apply.</p><h2>Your rights</h2><p>Subject to the applicable conditions, you may request access, rectification, erasure, restriction of processing or data portability. You may object to processing based on legitimate interests. Where processing relies on consent, you may withdraw it for the future.</p><p>You may also complain to a data protection supervisory authority. For Baden-Württemberg, see the <a href=\\\"https://www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de/\\\">state data protection authority</a>. Contact the controller above to exercise your rights.</p><h2>Reusing the starter kit</h2><p>This notice describes this demonstration, not every project built from the repository. If you reuse Craft Kit, adapt the notice to your controller details, hosting, enabled integrations and actual data-processing configuration.</p><p>Last updated: 13 November 2026.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-13 15:56:26','2026-11-13 15:56:26','8d3b0c01-7f0f-4f45-86c7-3c45bac94da3'),(2828,2828,1,'Privacy policy','privacy-policy','privacy-policy','{\"1a9a9160-4064-4d41-b55f-e820ab14f99c\": 0, \"872f39e9-c6e7-4db6-9915-33944177d829\": \"Privacy policy\", \"981facbf-3e2f-4e4b-9f37-d1b8629dd72b\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">Craft Kit is a public demonstration of a Craft CMS starter kit and its Datastar examples. It also presents my work as an independent developer. This notice explains how personal data is processed when you visit or use the demos.</p><h2>Controller and contact</h2><p>Andi Grether<br />Badstr. 22c<br />79379 Müllheim<br />Germany<br /><a href=\\\"mailto:webworker.ag@gmail.com\\\">webworker.ag@gmail.com</a></p><h2>Website delivery and server logs</h2><p>When you request a page or an asset, the hosting system processes technical information such as your IP address, request URL, time of access, browser information and response status. These data are needed to deliver the website, diagnose problems and protect it from abuse.</p><p>Logs are retained only as long as needed for these purposes or to investigate security incidents, subject to applicable retention obligations. The legal basis for operating and securing the website is our legitimate interest under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR.</p><h2>Datastar interactions, search and the Todo List</h2><p>Datastar is served from this website. Navigation controls, search, filters and the demo forms send requests to this website when a server response is needed. Search terms and submitted demo values are processed to provide the requested result. They can also form part of the requested URL and technical access logs.</p><p>The Todo List stores tasks in your server-side session. A necessary session cookie can associate your browser with that session; security cookies may also be used for request protection. The demo does not create a permanent personal task account. Do not enter sensitive or confidential information into it.</p><p>Essential session and security storage is used to provide the function you request, not for advertising. The applicable basis is Article 6(1)(f) GDPR and, for strictly necessary device storage or access, Section 25(2) TDDDG.</p><h2>Cookieless Matomo analytics</h2><p>The public website uses a self-hosted Matomo installation at <strong>p-e8qn5a.project.space</strong> to understand use of the demo. The tracker is configured with cookies disabled. It records page views and link interactions; the demos also send event categories such as adding or completing a task and opening the Bitcoin data panel. The task text is not included in those custom event calls.</p><p>Analytics can process the visited URL, referrer, time of access, browser and device information, and the IP address received by the analytics server. Cookieless tracking is still data processing. IP masking and retention are controlled in the Matomo server configuration; disabling cookies alone is not a guarantee of anonymity.</p><p>The purpose is to improve the demonstration and understand which examples are useful, based on our legitimate interest under Article 6(1)(f) GDPR. You may object to processing based on legitimate interests by contacting the controller. If a particular analytics configuration requires consent, it must be assessed separately; the absence of cookies does not decide that question by itself.</p><h2>OpenStreetMap on the map demo</h2><p>Opening the <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map demo</a> loads map tiles directly from OpenStreetMap tile servers. These requests transmit technical connection data, including your IP address and the requested tile URLs, to the map service. This is separate from the locally served Leaflet code used to display the map.</p><p>The map is provided to demonstrate location filtering. Details about the service\'s data processing are available in the <a href=\\\"https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy\\\">OpenStreetMap Foundation privacy policy</a>. Other pages do not need to load those map tiles.</p><h2>AI-generated content</h2><p>All editorial texts and illustrations on this demo website were generated or revised using artificial intelligence. Actual screenshots of the Craft control panel are not AI-generated. Generated illustrations are identified by an AI badge.</p><p>AI-assisted content may contain errors. It is provided to demonstrate the starter kit and does not replace independent professional advice. Simply viewing the texts and images does not send your data to an AI service.</p><h2>Images, fonts and frontend assets</h2><p>The demo images, fonts and application UI scripts are served from this website. The Matomo analytics script is loaded separately from the analytics host described above. The editorial illustrations were created for Craft Kit and are marked with an AI badge. Viewing them does not send an image-generation request or visitor data to an AI image service. Unsplash images and remote font services are not used.</p><h2>Server-side data sources and external links</h2><p>The GitHub repository statistics and Binance BTC/USDT ticker are fetched by the website\'s server. The demo does not send your search terms or task text to those services, and your browser does not need to contact their APIs to display the results.</p><p>Links to GitHub, LinkedIn, documentation and external articles take you to other websites when you follow them. Their operators then process data under their own privacy policies.</p><h2>Contact by email</h2><p>If you send an email, your message and contact details are processed to answer your inquiry. This includes processing by the email service providers involved in delivery. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(b) GDPR for contract-related inquiries, or Article 6(1)(f) GDPR for other correspondence. Messages are deleted when no longer needed, unless legal retention obligations apply.</p><h2>Your rights</h2><p>Subject to the applicable conditions, you may request access, rectification, erasure, restriction of processing or data portability. You may object to processing based on legitimate interests. Where processing relies on consent, you may withdraw it for the future.</p><p>You may also complain to a data protection supervisory authority. For Baden-Württemberg, see the <a href=\\\"https://www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de/\\\">state data protection authority</a>. Contact the controller above to exercise your rights.</p><h2>Reusing the starter kit</h2><p>This notice describes this demonstration, not every project built from the repository. If you reuse Craft Kit, adapt the notice to your controller details, hosting, enabled integrations and actual data-processing configuration.</p><p>Last updated: 13 November 2026.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-15 14:14:40','2026-11-15 14:14:40','15dceb50-a42c-48f8-bbaf-cce6e6c977f2'),(2829,2829,1,'Datastar in Craft — one reactive model for navigation, search and filters',NULL,NULL,'{\"48d2238f-4200-4601-8702-9f2157892222\": \"AI-generated illustration for Craft Kit\", \"5ab4cacf-3877-4008-802e-aa67b2627478\": true, \"e30c17b9-ffa5-4343-99ee-0f780e5e0ed0\": \"Navigation, search and filters connected by one reactive model.\"}',1,'2026-11-15 14:24:38','2026-11-15 14:24:38','f09144a5-6e2b-490e-b4b1-30d0fc4138bc'),(2830,2830,1,'Datastar in Craft CMS: Live Search, Filters and Navigation with Twig','datastar-craft-cms-live-search-filters-navigation-twig','blog/datastar-craft-cms-live-search-filters-navigation-twig','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-11-15 00:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Datastar in Craft CMS: Live Search, Filters and Navigation with Twig\"}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','49c6a54e-02f2-4442-8c32-c799ecbbd3cb'),(2831,2831,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Build live search, filters and navigation with Craft CMS, Twig and Datastar. Follow real examples from Craft Kit, with one reactive model and no Alpine layer.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2829]}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','fefdec30-bcdb-480b-912f-b59d2470f618'),(2832,2832,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">The useful test of a frontend stack is not whether it can build one impressive demo. It is whether the ordinary interactions fit together: opening the navigation, revealing a search panel, typing a query and filtering a list.</p><p>Craft Kit uses Datastar for all of those interactions. There is no Alpine layer for the small pieces and a different framework for server requests. Craft renders the HTML, and one reactive model connects the interface.</p><p>This article walks through that model using examples from the kit. The snippets are shortened for readability; links to the complete templates are included below. If you want the comparison first, read <a href=\\\"{entry:1487@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog/datastar-vs-alpine-htmx-why-datastar-is-the-better-fit-for-craft-cms}\\\">why I prefer Datastar to an Alpine and HTMX combination for Craft</a>.</p><h2>One model, two kinds of state</h2><p>An open dropdown and a saved entry are not the same kind of state. The dropdown can live in the browser. The entry belongs to Craft, together with its permissions and validation.</p><p>Datastar signals handle the temporary values: whether the menu is open, what is in the search input, which category is selected. When those values need a server response, Datastar sends a request and applies the returned HTML. A local toggle does not need a round trip.</p><p>That is the foundation: <strong>browser signals for the interaction, Craft for authoritative data, Twig for the response.</strong></p><h2>1. Start with the navigation and search panel</h2><p>The base template defines a shared <strong>ui</strong> namespace for site-wide controls. This is the actual shape used by the kit:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">&lt;body\\n  data-signals=\\\"{ui: {\\n    searchOpen: false,\\n    navOpen: false,\\n    dropdownOpen: false,\\n    btcOpen: false\\n  }}\\\"\\n&gt;</code></pre><p>The search button toggles a signal and closes the mobile navigation. The panel reacts to the same signal. With styling and icon markup removed, the interaction looks like this:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">&lt;button\\n  type=\\\"button\\\"\\n  data-on:click=\\\"$ui.searchOpen = !$ui.searchOpen;\\n    $ui.navOpen = false\\\"\\n  data-attr=\\\"{\\n    \'aria-expanded\': $ui.searchOpen ? \'true\' : \'false\'\\n  }\\\"\\n&gt;\\n  Search\\n&lt;/button&gt;\\n\\n&lt;div\\n  class=\\\"hidden\\\"\\n  data-class=\\\"{\\n    \'block\': $ui.searchOpen,\\n    \'hidden\': !$ui.searchOpen\\n  }\\\"\\n&gt;\\n  {# Search component goes here #}\\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p>There is no endpoint here because no server data needs to change. Datastar updates the classes and the button\'s expanded state in the browser.</p><p>Navigation dropdowns use the same store. The kit keeps the open dropdown\'s entry ID in <strong>ui.dropdownOpen</strong>, rather than creating an unrelated state system for each menu. The navigation template also handles outside clicks and Escape.</p><p>See <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_globals/nav.twig\\\">the navigation template</a> and <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_partials/entry/compNavigationLabel.twig\\\">the dropdown component</a>. Use the menu on this page to try the behavior.</p><h2>2. Turn a search term into a Twig request</h2><p>Live search adds the next step: the browser has a value, but Craft owns the searchable content. The search component binds the input to <strong>ui.search</strong> and requests a Twig endpoint after a short debounce.</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set type = type ?? \'page\' %}\\n{% set fetchAction = datastar.get(\\n  \'_datastar/search.twig\', {type: type}\\n) %}\\n\\n&lt;div data-signals=\\\"{ui: {search: \'\'}}\\\"&gt;\\n  &lt;input\\n    type=\\\"search\\\"\\n    aria-label=\\\"Search\\\"\\n    data-bind:ui.search\\n    data-on:input__debounce.300ms=\\\"{{ fetchAction }}\\\"\\n    data-indicator:fetching\\n  /&gt;\\n  &lt;div id=\\\"searchresult-{{ type }}\\\"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p><strong>data-bind</strong> keeps the input and signal together. <strong>data-on:input__debounce.300ms</strong> waits until typing pauses before running the request expression. <strong>data-indicator</strong> exposes the request\'s loading state as a signal.</p><p>The Craft Datastar plugin creates the request expression through <strong>datastar.get()</strong>. You point it at a template; you do not need to add a JSON API or a separate frontend route for the result.</p><p>The <strong>type</strong> variable distinguishes the full search page from the search panel. Their result containers have different IDs, so a response can update the intended instance.</p><h2>3. Let Craft query and render the results</h2><p>On the server, the search term is available through <strong>signals.ui.search</strong>. The endpoint queries Craft entries and renders links with Twig. This shortened version keeps the same query and target-ID pattern as the kit:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set type = type ?? \'page\' %}\\n{% set search = signals.ui.search ?? \'\' %}\\n{% set query = craft.entries.section([\'blog\', \'pages\']) %}\\n{% set entries = search\\n  ? query.search(search)\\n    .orderBy(\'score desc, postDate desc\')\\n    .limit(100).all()\\n  : []\\n%}\\n\\n{% patchelements %}\\n  &lt;div id=\\\"searchresult-{{ type }}\\\"&gt;\\n    {% for result in entries %}\\n      &lt;a href=\\\"{{ result.url }}\\\"&gt;{{ result.title }}&lt;/a&gt;\\n    {% else %}\\n      &lt;p&gt;No matching content yet.&lt;/p&gt;\\n    {% endfor %}\\n  &lt;/div&gt;\\n{% endpatchelements %}</code></pre><p>The important connection is the matching <strong>searchresult-{{ type }}</strong> ID in the component and response. The plugin\'s <strong>patchelements</strong> tag returns an HTML update, and Datastar morphs it into the page.</p><p>The complete endpoint has separate messages for an empty query and a query with no results. Keep those states useful: a blank container should not be the only feedback after someone searches.</p><p>Try <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">live search</a>, then compare <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_partials/entry/etSearch.twig\\\">the search component</a> with <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_datastar/search.twig\\\">its Twig endpoint</a>. The request path is visible from the markup.</p><h2>4. Scope reusable filters to their entry</h2><p>A page-wide search value is intentionally shared. A reusable blog block should not accidentally share its selected category with another instance. The kit gives entry-based components a namespace derived from their entry ID.</p><p>The blog block uses <strong>app&lt;entry ID&gt;.filter</strong>, and passes its block ID to the endpoint:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set blockId = entry.id %}\\n{% set fetchAction = datastar.get(\\n  \'_datastar/blog.twig\', {offset: 0, entryId: blockId}\\n) %}\\n\\n&lt;div data-signals=\\\"{{\\n  {(\'app\' ~ blockId): {filter: \'\'}}|json_encode\\n}}\\\"&gt;\\n  &lt;select\\n    aria-label=\\\"Filter posts by category\\\"\\n    data-bind:app{{ blockId }}.filter\\n    data-on:change=\\\"{{ fetchAction }}\\\"\\n    data-indicator:blog_fetching\\n    data-attr:disabled=\\\"$blog_fetching\\\"\\n  &gt;\\n    {# Category options go here #}\\n  &lt;/select&gt;\\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p>The full template initializes the filter from the URL, renders the category options and loads the first set of entries on the server. The endpoint reads the matching signal namespace, resolves the category and runs the entry query.</p><p>The result container is scoped too: <strong>result-&lt;entry ID&gt;</strong>. Signals and DOM targets follow the same ownership boundary. That makes the block easier to reuse without a new naming convention for every feature.</p><h2>5. Use the same endpoint for filtering and load more</h2><p>The blog endpoint already knows the selected filter and pagination offset. A new filter replaces the current results; the load-more button appends the next entries. In the successful response, the difference is a patch mode:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set entryId = entryId ?? null %}\\n{% set filter = entryId\\n  ? (signals[\'app\' ~ entryId].filter ?? \'\')\\n  : \'\'\\n%}\\n\\n{# Successful response, after querying the entries #}\\n{% patchelements with {\\n  selector: \'#result-\' ~ entryId,\\n  mode: offset == 0 ? \'inner\' : \'append\'\\n} %}\\n  {% for entry in entries %}\\n    {{ entry.render() }}\\n  {% endfor %}\\n{% endpatchelements %}</code></pre><p>The endpoint also returns an updated load-more control, or removes it when there are no further entries. The query fetches one extra entry to determine whether another page is available.</p><p>That is still one feature expressed as Craft queries and Twig output. There is no parallel list renderer in a client-side component and no need to recreate the card markup in JavaScript.</p><p>Try the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog filters and load-more button</a>. The source lives in <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_partials/entry/blockBlog.twig\\\">blockBlog.twig</a> and <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_datastar/blog.twig\\\">the blog endpoint</a>.</p><h2>Loading states, URLs and keyboard access still matter</h2><p>A request indicator is not just a spinner. The blog also uses it to disable its filter and load-more button while a request is active. Clear loading and empty states make the interaction understandable.</p><p>Shareable URLs deserve the same attention. Craft Kit uses its <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_macros/datastar.twig\\\">urlSync macro</a> to synchronize selected signals with query parameters using <strong>history.replaceState</strong>. The blog reads its filter on the initial server request; the search page restores its query through the macro. This is not a promise of a complete browser-history or no-JavaScript implementation.</p><p>Use real links for navigation, give controls accessible names and test focus and Escape behavior. Datastar supplies reactive primitives; it does not automatically make every component accessible or give every interaction a useful no-JavaScript path.</p><h2>What the server still owns</h2><ul><li><strong>Validation:</strong> signals and URL parameters are untrusted input. Validate types, allowed values and query limits.</li><li><strong>Permissions:</strong> only query and mutate content the current visitor is allowed to use.</li><li><strong>Mutations:</strong> use POST, server-side validation and CSRF protection for changes, not a GET request that saves data.</li><li><strong>Escaping:</strong> keep Twig auto-escaping enabled. Returned HTML is not permission to trust arbitrary input.</li><li><strong>Caching:</strong> do not reuse personalized or filter-dependent output under a cache key that ignores those values.</li></ul><p>An SSE response can finish after sending its patches. These examples do not require a permanent connection or a dedicated streaming server. Long-lived updates are a separate hosting and application decision.</p><h2>No Alpine layer does not mean no JavaScript</h2><p>Datastar runs JavaScript in the browser, and its attribute expressions describe browser behavior. The benefit is not pretending otherwise. It is keeping everyday UI behavior and server requests in one model, close to the HTML and Twig that developers already work with.</p><p>The kit still uses Leaflet for the map, Embla for the slider and GLightbox for galleries. Those are specialist integrations, not a second general-purpose UI framework competing with Datastar for the same state.</p><h2>Build your next interaction from a working example</h2><p>Start with the navigation, follow a search request into its endpoint, then inspect how the blog scopes its filter and appends another page. The <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a> shows server-confirmed mutations, and the <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map</a> shows filtered content feeding a specialist browser library.</p><p>The <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}\\\">Builder demo</a> covers the editorial side: reusable Twig components rendered from Matrix and CKEditor.</p><p><strong>Craft Kit is the working starting point.</strong> <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Download it on GitHub</a>, explore the examples and keep the parts that fit your project. For the integration details, use the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\">Craft Datastar plugin documentation</a> and the <a href=\\\"https://data-star.dev/reference/attributes\\\">Datastar attribute reference</a>.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','beba3218-7b0e-43f6-a690-21a9e84747e2'),(2833,2833,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Datastar in Craft CMS: Search, Filters & Navigation with Twig\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Build live search, filters and navigation with Craft CMS, Twig and Datastar. Follow real examples from Craft Kit, with one reactive model and no Alpine layer.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2829]}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','2a1dd4bc-b3b8-4da6-a73d-ce756508b858'),(2834,2834,1,'Datastar in Craft CMS: Live Search, Filters and Navigation with Twig','datastar-craft-cms-live-search-filters-navigation-twig','blog/datastar-craft-cms-live-search-filters-navigation-twig','{\"6268711e-041f-4ba2-ac1f-69075f35f133\": {\"date\": \"2026-11-15 00:00:00\"}, \"6ebd48e4-16ae-438b-88db-b7c32dac5dbd\": [892], \"d74fbbee-f4c8-448b-b8fc-d5723a6d0f07\": 0, \"f333c3f6-b95e-4720-8b85-bac84b0fd6bf\": \"Datastar in Craft CMS: Live Search, Filters and Navigation with Twig\"}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','0b217e2f-7df1-4f41-b132-ae660dcaf4d5'),(2835,2835,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"41d42c36-230e-4e28-96f8-8a4801e9694e\": \"<p>Build live search, filters and navigation with Craft CMS, Twig and Datastar. Follow real examples from Craft Kit, with one reactive model and no Alpine layer.</p>\", \"914ae46d-5dd7-4afd-b5a3-32fbed3aecae\": [2829]}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','e140dd56-b824-4f97-aa96-fa6af83319c7'),(2836,2836,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"54ea783f-26e8-4a98-a119-9a041012aa68\": \"<p class=\\\"ui-teaser\\\">The useful test of a frontend stack is not whether it can build one impressive demo. It is whether the ordinary interactions fit together: opening the navigation, revealing a search panel, typing a query and filtering a list.</p><p>Craft Kit uses Datastar for all of those interactions. There is no Alpine layer for the small pieces and a different framework for server requests. Craft renders the HTML, and one reactive model connects the interface.</p><p>This article walks through that model using examples from the kit. The snippets are shortened for readability; links to the complete templates are included below. If you want the comparison first, read <a href=\\\"{entry:1487@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog/datastar-vs-alpine-htmx-why-datastar-is-the-better-fit-for-craft-cms}\\\">why I prefer Datastar to an Alpine and HTMX combination for Craft</a>.</p><h2>One model, two kinds of state</h2><p>An open dropdown and a saved entry are not the same kind of state. The dropdown can live in the browser. The entry belongs to Craft, together with its permissions and validation.</p><p>Datastar signals handle the temporary values: whether the menu is open, what is in the search input, which category is selected. When those values need a server response, Datastar sends a request and applies the returned HTML. A local toggle does not need a round trip.</p><p>That is the foundation: <strong>browser signals for the interaction, Craft for authoritative data, Twig for the response.</strong></p><h2>1. Start with the navigation and search panel</h2><p>The base template defines a shared <strong>ui</strong> namespace for site-wide controls. This is the actual shape used by the kit:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">&lt;body\\n  data-signals=\\\"{ui: {\\n    searchOpen: false,\\n    navOpen: false,\\n    dropdownOpen: false,\\n    btcOpen: false\\n  }}\\\"\\n&gt;</code></pre><p>The search button toggles a signal and closes the mobile navigation. The panel reacts to the same signal. With styling and icon markup removed, the interaction looks like this:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">&lt;button\\n  type=\\\"button\\\"\\n  data-on:click=\\\"$ui.searchOpen = !$ui.searchOpen;\\n    $ui.navOpen = false\\\"\\n  data-attr=\\\"{\\n    \'aria-expanded\': $ui.searchOpen ? \'true\' : \'false\'\\n  }\\\"\\n&gt;\\n  Search\\n&lt;/button&gt;\\n\\n&lt;div\\n  class=\\\"hidden\\\"\\n  data-class=\\\"{\\n    \'block\': $ui.searchOpen,\\n    \'hidden\': !$ui.searchOpen\\n  }\\\"\\n&gt;\\n  {# Search component goes here #}\\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p>There is no endpoint here because no server data needs to change. Datastar updates the classes and the button\'s expanded state in the browser.</p><p>Navigation dropdowns use the same store. The kit keeps the open dropdown\'s entry ID in <strong>ui.dropdownOpen</strong>, rather than creating an unrelated state system for each menu. The navigation template also handles outside clicks and Escape.</p><p>See <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_globals/nav.twig\\\">the navigation template</a> and <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_partials/entry/compNavigationLabel.twig\\\">the dropdown component</a>. Use the menu on this page to try the behavior.</p><h2>2. Turn a search term into a Twig request</h2><p>Live search adds the next step: the browser has a value, but Craft owns the searchable content. The search component binds the input to <strong>ui.search</strong> and requests a Twig endpoint after a short debounce.</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set type = type ?? \'page\' %}\\n{% set fetchAction = datastar.get(\\n  \'_datastar/search.twig\', {type: type}\\n) %}\\n\\n&lt;div data-signals=\\\"{ui: {search: \'\'}}\\\"&gt;\\n  &lt;input\\n    type=\\\"search\\\"\\n    aria-label=\\\"Search\\\"\\n    data-bind:ui.search\\n    data-on:input__debounce.300ms=\\\"{{ fetchAction }}\\\"\\n    data-indicator:fetching\\n  /&gt;\\n  &lt;div id=\\\"searchresult-{{ type }}\\\"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p><strong>data-bind</strong> keeps the input and signal together. <strong>data-on:input__debounce.300ms</strong> waits until typing pauses before running the request expression. <strong>data-indicator</strong> exposes the request\'s loading state as a signal.</p><p>The Craft Datastar plugin creates the request expression through <strong>datastar.get()</strong>. You point it at a template; you do not need to add a JSON API or a separate frontend route for the result.</p><p>The <strong>type</strong> variable distinguishes the full search page from the search panel. Their result containers have different IDs, so a response can update the intended instance.</p><h2>3. Let Craft query and render the results</h2><p>On the server, the search term is available through <strong>signals.ui.search</strong>. The endpoint queries Craft entries and renders links with Twig. This shortened version keeps the same query and target-ID pattern as the kit:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set type = type ?? \'page\' %}\\n{% set search = signals.ui.search ?? \'\' %}\\n{% set query = craft.entries.section([\'blog\', \'pages\']) %}\\n{% set entries = search\\n  ? query.search(search)\\n    .orderBy(\'score desc, postDate desc\')\\n    .limit(100).all()\\n  : []\\n%}\\n\\n{% patchelements %}\\n  &lt;div id=\\\"searchresult-{{ type }}\\\"&gt;\\n    {% for result in entries %}\\n      &lt;a href=\\\"{{ result.url }}\\\"&gt;{{ result.title }}&lt;/a&gt;\\n    {% else %}\\n      &lt;p&gt;No matching content yet.&lt;/p&gt;\\n    {% endfor %}\\n  &lt;/div&gt;\\n{% endpatchelements %}</code></pre><p>The important connection is the matching <strong>searchresult-{{ type }}</strong> ID in the component and response. The plugin\'s <strong>patchelements</strong> tag returns an HTML update, and Datastar morphs it into the page.</p><p>The complete endpoint has separate messages for an empty query and a query with no results. Keep those states useful: a blank container should not be the only feedback after someone searches.</p><p>Try <a href=\\\"{entry:723@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/search}\\\">live search</a>, then compare <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_partials/entry/etSearch.twig\\\">the search component</a> with <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_datastar/search.twig\\\">its Twig endpoint</a>. The request path is visible from the markup.</p><h2>4. Scope reusable filters to their entry</h2><p>A page-wide search value is intentionally shared. A reusable blog block should not accidentally share its selected category with another instance. The kit gives entry-based components a namespace derived from their entry ID.</p><p>The blog block uses <strong>app&lt;entry ID&gt;.filter</strong>, and passes its block ID to the endpoint:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set blockId = entry.id %}\\n{% set fetchAction = datastar.get(\\n  \'_datastar/blog.twig\', {offset: 0, entryId: blockId}\\n) %}\\n\\n&lt;div data-signals=\\\"{{\\n  {(\'app\' ~ blockId): {filter: \'\'}}|json_encode\\n}}\\\"&gt;\\n  &lt;select\\n    aria-label=\\\"Filter posts by category\\\"\\n    data-bind:app{{ blockId }}.filter\\n    data-on:change=\\\"{{ fetchAction }}\\\"\\n    data-indicator:blog_fetching\\n    data-attr:disabled=\\\"$blog_fetching\\\"\\n  &gt;\\n    {# Category options go here #}\\n  &lt;/select&gt;\\n&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre><p>The full template initializes the filter from the URL, renders the category options and loads the first set of entries on the server. The endpoint reads the matching signal namespace, resolves the category and runs the entry query.</p><p>The result container is scoped too: <strong>result-&lt;entry ID&gt;</strong>. Signals and DOM targets follow the same ownership boundary. That makes the block easier to reuse without a new naming convention for every feature.</p><h2>5. Use the same endpoint for filtering and load more</h2><p>The blog endpoint already knows the selected filter and pagination offset. A new filter replaces the current results; the load-more button appends the next entries. In the successful response, the difference is a patch mode:</p><pre><code class=\\\"language-twig\\\">{% set entryId = entryId ?? null %}\\n{% set filter = entryId\\n  ? (signals[\'app\' ~ entryId].filter ?? \'\')\\n  : \'\'\\n%}\\n\\n{# Successful response, after querying the entries #}\\n{% patchelements with {\\n  selector: \'#result-\' ~ entryId,\\n  mode: offset == 0 ? \'inner\' : \'append\'\\n} %}\\n  {% for entry in entries %}\\n    {{ entry.render() }}\\n  {% endfor %}\\n{% endpatchelements %}</code></pre><p>The endpoint also returns an updated load-more control, or removes it when there are no further entries. The query fetches one extra entry to determine whether another page is available.</p><p>That is still one feature expressed as Craft queries and Twig output. There is no parallel list renderer in a client-side component and no need to recreate the card markup in JavaScript.</p><p>Try the <a href=\\\"{entry:258@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/blog}\\\">blog filters and load-more button</a>. The source lives in <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_partials/entry/blockBlog.twig\\\">blockBlog.twig</a> and <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_datastar/blog.twig\\\">the blog endpoint</a>.</p><h2>Loading states, URLs and keyboard access still matter</h2><p>A request indicator is not just a spinner. The blog also uses it to disable its filter and load-more button while a request is active. Clear loading and empty states make the interaction understandable.</p><p>Shareable URLs deserve the same attention. Craft Kit uses its <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit/blob/main/templates/_macros/datastar.twig\\\">urlSync macro</a> to synchronize selected signals with query parameters using <strong>history.replaceState</strong>. The blog reads its filter on the initial server request; the search page restores its query through the macro. This is not a promise of a complete browser-history or no-JavaScript implementation.</p><p>Use real links for navigation, give controls accessible names and test focus and Escape behavior. Datastar supplies reactive primitives; it does not automatically make every component accessible or give every interaction a useful no-JavaScript path.</p><h2>What the server still owns</h2><ul><li><strong>Validation:</strong> signals and URL parameters are untrusted input. Validate types, allowed values and query limits.</li><li><strong>Permissions:</strong> only query and mutate content the current visitor is allowed to use.</li><li><strong>Mutations:</strong> use POST, server-side validation and CSRF protection for changes, not a GET request that saves data.</li><li><strong>Escaping:</strong> keep Twig auto-escaping enabled. Returned HTML is not permission to trust arbitrary input.</li><li><strong>Caching:</strong> do not reuse personalized or filter-dependent output under a cache key that ignores those values.</li></ul><p>An SSE response can finish after sending its patches. These examples do not require a permanent connection or a dedicated streaming server. Long-lived updates are a separate hosting and application decision.</p><h2>No Alpine layer does not mean no JavaScript</h2><p>Datastar runs JavaScript in the browser, and its attribute expressions describe browser behavior. The benefit is not pretending otherwise. It is keeping everyday UI behavior and server requests in one model, close to the HTML and Twig that developers already work with.</p><p>The kit still uses Leaflet for the map, Embla for the slider and GLightbox for galleries. Those are specialist integrations, not a second general-purpose UI framework competing with Datastar for the same state.</p><h2>Build your next interaction from a working example</h2><p>Start with the navigation, follow a search request into its endpoint, then inspect how the blog scopes its filter and appends another page. The <a href=\\\"/hypermedia-todolist-craft-cms-datastar\\\">Todo List</a> shows server-confirmed mutations, and the <a href=\\\"{entry:1029@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/map}\\\">map</a> shows filtered content feeding a specialist browser library.</p><p>The <a href=\\\"{entry:99@1:url||https://craftcms-lazy.ddev.site/builder}\\\">Builder demo</a> covers the editorial side: reusable Twig components rendered from Matrix and CKEditor.</p><p><strong>Craft Kit is the working starting point.</strong> <a href=\\\"https://github.com/handplant/craftcms-lazy-starter-kit\\\">Download it on GitHub</a>, explore the examples and keep the parts that fit your project. For the integration details, use the <a href=\\\"https://putyourlightson.com/plugins/datastar\\\">Craft Datastar plugin documentation</a> and the <a href=\\\"https://data-star.dev/reference/attributes\\\">Datastar attribute reference</a>.</p>\"}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','2d849c1f-0977-4278-9fa9-6634757a5474'),(2837,2837,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"9bd2388d-d293-4f1b-8283-61a80c29e4ac\": \"Datastar in Craft CMS: Search, Filters & Navigation with Twig\", \"e3b0b587-97fc-47a0-a491-7e55f71b27ad\": false, \"f9112d3e-b4f8-4c57-bbba-1c369f98698d\": \"Build live search, filters and navigation with Craft CMS, Twig and Datastar. Follow real examples from Craft Kit, with one reactive model and no Alpine layer.\", \"fb364fbf-e22b-4dbe-8570-724df7c5e068\": [2829]}',1,'2026-11-15 14:27:22','2026-11-15 14:27:22','6232e346-50b1-4ea9-90ac-503b370aa76a');
