headline and teaser with an optional image and link add and reorder the nested card entries inside the block view the card block '),(1373,'slug',0,1,' temp zddmiezhcowhvvycomtuzzqdacozlmohhvhm '),(1373,'title',0,1,''),(1374,'field',8,1,' accordions '),(1374,'field',20,1,' you are using an accordion block now each item has a heading rich text and an initial open or closed state use it for faqs or supporting details that readers can expand when needed '),(1374,'slug',0,1,' temp cmrnwpfqtqzgswipneamgkxvibjomlcrazfw '),(1374,'title',0,1,''),(1375,'field',8,1,' gallery '),(1375,'field',20,1,' select and arrange several images in one block the page shows a preview grid opening an image launches the lightbox with the full selection view the gallery block '),(1375,'slug',0,1,' temp arkvvvzawwxwegqkblwutjidbidtphinbouf '),(1375,'title',0,1,''),(1376,'field',8,1,' block code '),(1376,'field',20,1,' a syntax highlighted block for showing code examples in a clean readable format scroll to block code '),(1376,'slug',0,1,' temp cgaohencpswluqycvptwgkijcwewujdalzfm '),(1376,'title',0,1,''),(1377,'field',8,1,' slider '),(1377,'field',20,1,' the slider presents nested card entries in an embla carousel use the arrow buttons or drag the cards to browse this example uses cards without images view the slider block '),(1377,'slug',0,1,' temp mzavqhbrjmontcfxiyhhjalhelokfemkpbii '),(1377,'title',0,1,''),(1378,'field',8,1,' call to action '),(1378,'field',20,1,' a call to action block combines a headline optional text and a link use it to give a section a clear next step view the call to action block '),(1378,'slug',0,1,' temp vdlnoxztodxfkbntzlzcmancspfjwhzxfinn '),(1378,'title',0,1,''),(1392,'slug',0,1,''),(1393,'slug',0,1,''),(1394,'slug',0,1,''),(1395,'slug',0,1,''),(1396,'slug',0,1,''),(1397,'slug',0,1,''),(1398,'slug',0,1,''),(1399,'slug',0,1,''),(1400,'slug',0,1,''),(1470,'slug',0,1,''),(1471,'slug',0,1,''),(1472,'slug',0,1,''),(1473,'slug',0,1,''),(1487,'field',8,1,' datastar vs alpine htmx why datastar is the better fit for craft cms '),(1487,'field',10,1,' development '),(1487,'field',17,1,''),(1487,'field',19,1,' when building dynamic craft cms frontends most developers reach for alpine js htmx its a popular combo and it works but after using both stacks on real craft projects i switched to datastar and havent looked back heres why the hypermedia renaissance for years the frontend world moved in one direction more javascript more complexity more frameworks react vue angular and with them build pipelines hydration strategies client side state management and a growing gap between backend and frontend then came a quiet counter movement htmx hotwire and others reminded developers that the web was built on hypermedia and that server rendered html sent over the wire is still a powerful and underrated approach datastar takes this further its not just less javascript its a complete rethink of where state lives how the dom updates and what the browser is actually good at the server drives the ui the browser renders it no spa overhead no hydration tax no javascript fatigue for craft cms developers this is a natural fit craft has always been server first twig first content first datastar speaks the same language the problem with sprig and alpine htmx in craft sprig brings htmx into craft as a first class plugin and it works well for isolated self contained components a search box here a filtered list there simple declarative twig friendly but sprig is built on htmxs fragment swap model every interaction replaces a chunk of html and the moment your ui grows shared state across components reactive forms live counters dynamic filters the cracks start to show no shared state components are isolated islands passing state between them requires workarounds like hidden inputs or url parameters fragile re initialization every fragment swap tears down and rebuilds the dom alpine loses its state on every update two libraries your glue bridging sprig and alpine requires custom events careful timing and code that shouldnt need to exist url query params fall out of sync filter and search state vanishes on page reload or when sharing a link infinite loop risk two components that need to react to each other quickly spiral into circular triggers solving it means wrestling with event propagation instead of building features out of band swaps dont scale updating multiple dom regions simultaneously requires hx swap oob scattering logic across templates and responses it works until it doesnt debugging is a guessing game when something breaks the question is always is this sprig alpine or a timing issue the more complex the project the more time you spend fighting the stack instead of building features a unified model changes everything datastar is not just another library its a unified model one tool that handles both server communication and client side reactivity built around the same server first philosophy that makes craft great no more two libraries patched together no more manual state bridging no more re initialization headaches signals originate on the server stay synchronized with the dom automatically and update exactly what changed nothing more craft renders twig datastar patches the result thats it why datastar fits craft cms better than sprig 1 shared state across the entire page sprig components are islands each component manages its own state independently sharing state between two sprig components requires creative workarounds hidden inputs url parameters or custom javascript datastar uses a global signal store that any element on the page can read and write a filter updated in one component instantly reflects everywhere else without a single line of custom javascript 2 dom morphing that doesnt break your ui when sprig swaps a fragment everything inside that fragment is torn down and rebuilt alpine components lose their state event listeners disappear nested interactions break datastar morphs the dom it patches exactly what changed at the node level state stays intact nothing breaks your ui stays alive 3 polling done right neither datastar nor sprig can do true server push with standard php hosting and thats okay but the way they handle polling is fundamentally different with sprig polling means re rendering an entire component on an interval every tick replaces the whole fragment regardless of whether anything actually changed datastars data on interval sends a lightweight request and patches only what changed the result feels more responsive generates less dom churn and is easier to reason about and when your hosting environment supports it sse is already built in ready to use without any extra setup 4 a natural next step from sprig if you know sprig datastar will feel immediately familiar server driven twig first no separate frontend app no build tools required the mental model is the same but datastar solves the problems sprig cant the learning curve is minimal the payoff is significant 5 one tool instead of two sprig handles server communication alpine handles client reactivity you handle everything in between datastar does both natively consistently without glue code what makes datastar fundamentally different these arent just feature differences theyre philosophical ones the server is the source of truth datastar is built around one core principle state belongs on the server the frontend is a reflection of server state not a parallel system that needs to be kept in sync with sprig alpine you inevitably end up managing state in two places with datastar there is only one place progressive enhancement by default datastar works with standard html forms and anchor tags page navigation is just a tags forms degrade gracefully you enhance progressively you dont replace the web platform you build on top of it sprig requires its own component model from the start fat morph send the whole page patch what changed datastars morphing engine lets you send large chunks of dom from the server even the entire html tag and it will patch only what actually changed no need to carefully manage fine grained fragment targets like in sprig less coordination fewer bugs cqrs pattern out of the box a long lived get request receives updates from the server short lived post requests send changes this simple pattern makes real time collaboration and live updates straightforward without websockets without polling hacks without extra infrastructure no optimistic ui lies datastar discourages optimistic updates that deceive the user show a loading indicator confirm from the server update the dom honest ux by design when sprig is still fine simple isolated interactions a toggle a basic component reload a single filtered list sprig handles perfectly if your project never needs shared state or real time updates sprig is a solid choice but the moment complexity grows datastar is the better long term investment a few things sprig simply cant do worth mentioning as a bonus some datastar features have no real equivalent in sprig at all data on intersect fires when an element enters the viewport perfect for lazy loaded content or scroll triggered requests no javascript needed data indicator automatically tracks request state for loading spinners and disabled buttons purely in html data computed creates reactive derived values that stay in sync automatically and debounce throttle are built directly into the event system as modifiers data on input debounce 300ms is all it takes for a live search input for url sync specifically data query string pro keeps signal values in sync with url parameters including browser history the exact pain point that sprig projects routinely work around with custom solutions small things but they add up see the full list of datastar attributes conclusion sprig and alpine are good tools but for modern craft cms projects datastar fits the architecture better server first twig native unified state real time out of the box less glue code fewer bugs more fun to build with datastar in craft cms made easy by ben croker getting started with datastar in craft is straightforward thanks to the craft datastar plugin by ben croker of putyourlightson the plugin handles the craft specific integration out of the box sse responses from twig templates action routing and all the wiring that would otherwise require custom boilerplate its the missing piece that makes datastar a first class citizen in craft just like sprig did for htmx a big thank you to ben croker for building and maintaining this without it the barrier to getting started with datastar in craft would be significantly higher putyourlightson com plugins datastar if you want to see datastar in action inside real craft cms projects take a look at the live demos at craft kit dev dynamic blog filtering and pagination without a page reload rick morty explorer reactive api driven ui built entirely in twig map filtering location based filtering with live updates live search instant search results server driven these examples show how datastar stays fast transparent and maintainable replacing the complexity of alpine htmx stacks with clean server first twig the full source code is available on github feel free to explore fork and use it as a starting point https github com handplant craftcms lazy starter kit for a deeper dive into the philosophy behind datastar read the tao of datastar https data star dev guide the tao of datastar for craft specific patterns and integration details visit the official docs https craftcms data star dev building a craft cms project and want to go the datastar route im andi grether a freelance craft cms developer specializing in exactly this stack happy to help webworker me '),(1487,'field',27,1,' datastar vs alpine htmx the better choice for craft cms why i choose datastar for craft cms shared signals dom morphing and twig driven updates with a practical comparison to sprig htmx and alpine '),(1487,'field',28,1,' why i choose datastar for craft cms shared signals dom morphing and twig driven updates with a practical comparison to sprig htmx and alpine '),(1487,'field',35,1,' freiburg im breisgau baden wurttemberg germany '),(1487,'slug',0,1,' datastar vs alpine htmx why datastar is the better fit for craft cms '),(1487,'title',0,1,' datastar vs alpine htmx why datastar is the better fit for craft cms '),(1488,'field',11,1,' why i choose datastar for craft cms shared signals dom morphing and twig driven updates with a practical comparison to sprig htmx and alpine '),(1488,'slug',0,1,''),(1489,'field',4,1,' datastar vs alpine htmx the better choice for craft cms why i choose datastar for craft cms shared signals dom morphing and twig driven updates with a practical comparison to sprig htmx and alpine '),(1489,'slug',0,1,''),(1490,'field',20,1,' when building dynamic craft cms frontends alpine js and htmx are familiar choices its a popular combo and it works but after using both stacks on real craft projects i switched to datastar and havent looked back heres why this site is the working example its navigation dropdowns mobile menu and search panel use datastar just like the blog filters and todo list there is no alpine layer for everyday ui state try the demos then inspect the twig templates on github want to put this into practice follow the craft and datastar guide to live search filters and navigation with short code examples from this starter kit the hypermedia renaissance for years the frontend world moved in one direction more javascript more complexity more frameworks react vue angular and with them build pipelines hydration strategies client side state management and a growing gap between backend and frontend then came a quiet counter movement htmx hotwire and others reminded developers that the web was built on hypermedia and that server rendered html sent over the wire is still a powerful and underrated approach datastar takes this further its not just less javascript its a complete rethink of where state lives how the dom updates and what the browser is actually good at the server drives the ui the browser renders it no spa overhead no hydration tax no javascript fatigue for craft cms developers this is a natural fit craft has always been server first twig first content first datastar speaks the same language where sprig and alpine need more coordination sprig brings htmx into craft as a first class plugin and it works well for isolated self contained components a search box here a filtered list there simple declarative twig friendly sprig uses htmx to re render twig components and update selected parts of the page that is a practical model when a page also has shared filters live counters and browser side interactions i have found that coordinating sprig and alpine can require more decisions state ownership decide which values belong in form fields url parameters alpine stores or the server dom boundaries replacement can affect local state and javascript widgets targeted swaps preservation and morphing extensions help but need deliberate setup component communication sprig supports refreshing other components and triggering events the refresh relationships still need to be designed shareable urls sprig supports push and replace url behavior filters must still be restored from those urls just as they must with datastar multiple updates out of band swaps are useful for my projects a shared signal model and morphing responses are often easier to coordinate these are architectural trade offs not things sprig cannot do my preference for datastar comes from reducing the amount of coordination i need to maintain a unified model changes everything datastar is not just another library its a unified model one tool that handles both server communication and client side reactivity built around the same server first philosophy that makes craft great for many interactions i can use one set of attributes instead of coordinating two libraries third party widgets still need careful initialization and cleanup signals hold reactive values in the browser and can be patched by the server craft remains responsible for authoritative data validation and permissions craft renders twig datastar patches the response into the page why datastar fits craft cms better than sprig 1 shared state across the entire page sprig has explicit component boundaries with form values url parameters and refresh events connecting them that can be a useful separation i prefer a shared signal store when several parts of the page need the same reactive values datastar provides a page wide signal store a filter value can be read by a results list a counter and a reset button without a separate state bridge i namespace signals so reusable components do not accidentally share state 2 dom morphing that doesnt break your ui a replacement swap can discard local state inside its target sprig also offers targeted replacement and preservation and its documentation describes using a morphing extension it is not limited to replacing every component in full datastar uses dom morphing for element patches by default it reconciles the returned html with the existing nodes which helps preserve browser state stable ids and clear ownership boundaries still matter especially around maps sliders and other javascript integrations 3 polling done right both sprig and datastar can poll an endpoint in datastar data on interval can run an expression that sends a request the server still performs its queries and renders the response morphing can reduce dom disruption but does not automatically reduce server work or response size datastar also supports streamed updates over server sent events long lived streams are possible with php but require suitable worker capacity timeouts and buffering settings a short request response interaction is often enough for a craft website persistent connections are an architectural choice not a requirement 4 a natural next step from sprig if you know sprig parts of datastar will feel familiar server rendered html twig templates and no separate frontend application signals and streamed responses introduce their own concepts so i would migrate a real feature first rather than rewrite a working project wholesale the payoff for me is a more consistent model across server requests and browser side reactivity 5 one tool instead of two sprig handles server communication alpine handles client reactivity you handle everything in between datastar does both natively consistently without glue code what makes datastar fundamentally different these arent just feature differences theyre philosophical ones the server is the source of truth i keep business rules saved content and permissions on the server datastar signals handle transient browser state such as an open panel or an unsent search term they are untrusted input not a substitute for server side validation sprig and alpine can follow the same principle datastar gives me a convenient way to implement it progressive enhancement by design datastar can enhance standard links and forms but a useful no javascript path is not automatic i provide real urls form actions and server rendered results first then enhance the interaction the same care is needed with sprig a button whose only behavior is a datastar expression will not work without javascript fat morph send the whole page patch what changed datastar can reconcile a larger html region rather than requiring every change to have its own tiny response target that can simplify template composition the full returned html still travels over the network so response size query performance and stable element ids still deserve attention a cqrs style pattern when you need it you can separate a long lived get stream for updates from short lived post requests for mutations this is a useful pattern for live interfaces but collaboration still needs backend event delivery authorization and a hosting setup that supports the streams datastar supplies the browser side primitives not that entire application architecture server confirmed mutations for content changes i prefer to show a loading state wait for validation and update the ui from the server response datastar supports this straightforwardly it is a design choice not a guarantee provided by the library errors and retries still need useful feedback when sprig is still a good choice simple isolated interactions a toggle a basic component reload a single filtered list sprig handles perfectly if your project never needs shared state or real time updates sprig is a solid choice a working sprig implementation does not need replacing just because another tool exists team experience existing components and project requirements matter i choose datastar when its shared signals and response model make the next feature simpler useful datastar attributes in one place several everyday behaviors are available through one consistent attribute system some have equivalents in htmx or alpine the advantage for me is using them together without another integration layer data on intersect fires when an element enters the viewport perfect for lazy loaded content or scroll triggered requests no javascript needed data indicator automatically tracks request state for loading spinners and disabled buttons purely in html data computed creates reactive derived values that stay in sync automatically and debounce throttle are built directly into the event system as modifiers data on input debounce 300ms adds a delay before an input handler runs a live search also needs a signal binding a request action and an endpoint for url sync specifically data query string pro keeps signal values in sync with url parameters including browser history a convenient option rather than a capability exclusive to datastar sprig also supports pushing and replacing urls small things but they add up see the full list of datastar attributes conclusion sprig and alpine are good tools but for modern craft cms projects datastar fits the architecture better server first twig native with shared signals and streaming support when the project needs it less glue code fewer bugs more fun to build with datastar in craft cms made easy by ben croker getting started with datastar in craft is straightforward thanks to the craft datastar plugin by ben croker of putyourlightson the plugin handles the craft specific integration out of the box sse responses from twig templates action routing and all the wiring that would otherwise require custom boilerplate its the missing piece that makes datastar a first class citizen in craft just like sprig did for htmx a big thank you to ben croker for building and maintaining this without it the barrier to getting started with datastar in craft would be significantly higher putyourlightson com plugins datastar if you want to see datastar in action inside real craft cms projects take a look at the live demos at craft kit dev dynamic blog filtering and pagination without a page reload todo list add complete and delete tasks with server confirmed updates map filtering location based filtering with live updates live search instant search results server driven these examples show how the starter kit puts the approach into practice try a demo then inspect its twig templates and datastar endpoints on github the builder demo also shows how matrix and ckeditor share the same component templates the full source code is available on github feel free to explore fork and use it as a starting point https github com handplant craftcms lazy starter kit for a deeper dive into the philosophy behind datastar read the tao of datastar https data star dev guide the tao of datastar for craft specific integration details use the datastar plugin documentation for independent craft examples see datastar craft cms demos for the comparison above the sprig documentation also covers preservation url updates and component refreshes building a craft cms project and want to go the datastar route im andi grether a freelance craft cms developer specializing in exactly this stack happy to help webworker me '),(1490,'slug',0,1,' temp bzahrhiwuwbnfrboqxprwycyvhqpwpuoxidu '),(1490,'title',0,1,''),(1491,'slug',0,1,''),(1492,'field',20,1,' if you want to see datastar in action inside real craft cms projects take a look at the live demos at craft kit dev including datastar powered search dynamic blog views map filtering and reactive ui patterns built entirely in twig these examples show how datastar stays fast transparent and maintainable while replacing the complexity of alpine htmx stacks for a deeper dive into the philosophy behind datastar read the tao of datastar https data star dev guide the tao of datastar and for craft specific patterns and integration details visit the official docs https craftcms data star dev '),(1492,'slug',0,1,' temp chulqjgeweaeszmekvyungcgdvpfsvnqxlaw '),(1492,'title',0,1,''),(1493,'slug',0,1,''),(1494,'alt',0,1,''),(1494,'extension',0,1,' jpg '),(1494,'filename',0,1,' growtika ygqmjh2uotg unsplash jpg '),(1494,'kind',0,1,' image '),(1494,'slug',0,1,''),(1494,'title',0,1,' growtika y g qmjh2u o tg unsplash '),(1510,'slug',0,1,' temp vgccdvbdefwezefmckhnaqopreqdtocgmzvk '),(1510,'title',0,1,''),(1511,'slug',0,1,''),(1533,'slug',0,1,' temp irbztbgangodtmmqucccbzsgohshzkiyxqgn '),(1533,'title',0,1,''),(1566,'field',12,1,' what is the ckeditor builder the ckeditor builder lets editors insert predefined components into rich text fields without writing html it keeps content structured and consistent while still giving editors flexibility why combine ckeditor with reusable blocks by embedding predefined blocks images ctas quotes embeds you get the best of two worlds editorial freedom strict design control every block renders the same way across all pages how does it work in craft kit the builder exposes a curated list of components directly inside ckeditor via dropdowns when selected the editor inserts a clean placeholder tag craft renders the final twig component automatically when should you use ckeditor builder blocks use blocks when you need rich structured content inside long form text articles guides landing pages blog posts they keep the content maintainable and scalable even after years of editing '),(1566,'slug',0,1,' temp lpbivrvoejeftqsjgcolrqdwnvnyqorneqys '),(1566,'title',0,1,''),(1567,'field',8,1,' what is the ckeditor builder '),(1567,'field',20,1,' the ckeditor builder lets editors insert predefined components into rich text fields without writing html it keeps content structured and consistent while still giving editors flexibility '),(1567,'slug',0,1,' temp crztbvnvokxeotlzkqkanlbnrnhuhedixcyp '),(1567,'title',0,1,''),(1568,'field',8,1,' why combine ckeditor with reusable blocks '),(1568,'field',20,1,' by embedding predefined blocks images ctas quotes embeds you get the best of two worlds editorial freedom strict design control every block renders the same way across all pages '),(1568,'slug',0,1,' temp hkkxluccqdxuiiqxgyiokbmclfehzuiahpzr '),(1568,'title',0,1,''),(1569,'field',8,1,' how does it work in craft kit '),(1569,'field',20,1,' the builder exposes a curated list of components directly inside ckeditor via dropdowns when selected the editor inserts a clean placeholder tag craft renders the final twig component automatically '),(1569,'slug',0,1,' temp ceaohztrqqcyytgylrgqmkziepgbqonihnsk '),(1569,'title',0,1,''),(1570,'field',8,1,' when should you use ckeditor builder blocks '),(1570,'field',20,1,' use blocks when you need rich structured content inside long form text articles guides landing pages blog posts they keep the content maintainable and scalable even after years of editing '),(1570,'slug',0,1,' temp rwyqkdxvrrapknmjbdtivhrnnhqosssnpqkr '),(1570,'title',0,1,''),(1571,'slug',0,1,''),(1594,'field',12,1,' what changes inside ckeditor the block is part of the rich text flow rather than a separate full width section it uses the inline wrapper and inherits the existing content width are these different components no matrix and ckeditor use the same block templates the rendering context selects the wrapper spacing and container behavior '),(1594,'slug',0,1,' temp nzwqilhprefitoykdnndmnkqygbsdbwgxmlx '),(1594,'title',0,1,''),(1595,'field',8,1,' what changes inside ckeditor '),(1595,'field',20,1,' the block is part of the rich text flow rather than a separate full width section it uses the inline wrapper and inherits the existing content width '),(1595,'slug',0,1,' temp xjgdvegodljmwlkksbqeiwwutbmnpsizxqjn '),(1595,'title',0,1,''),(1596,'field',8,1,' are these different components '),(1596,'field',20,1,' no matrix and ckeditor use the same block templates the rendering context selects the wrapper spacing and container behavior '),(1596,'slug',0,1,' temp gtgqhtedokfasrmhfbzkjfczvkqzrvmvlqdc '),(1596,'title',0,1,''),(1597,'field',8,1,' how does it work in craft kit '),(1597,'field',20,1,' the builder exposes a curated list of components directly inside ckeditor via dropdowns when selected the editor inserts a clean placeholder tag craft renders the final twig component automatically '),(1597,'slug',0,1,' temp kzagrrtrbyhtnpprsqbwlfwjhzzvandrnvzd '),(1597,'title',0,1,''),(1598,'field',8,1,' when should you use ckeditor builder blocks '),(1598,'field',20,1,' use blocks when you need rich structured content inside long form text articles guides landing pages blog posts they keep the content maintainable and scalable even after years of editing '),(1598,'slug',0,1,' temp kctyojbprlqwbxxuemaghrqnpbpdphezigiw '),(1598,'title',0,1,''),(1599,'slug',0,1,''),(1611,'slug',0,1,' temp igbljwmkmxvycgserlgkiqwjofuiskhrquip '),(1611,'title',0,1,''),(1612,'slug',0,1,''),(1623,'field',7,1,' datastar blog try category filtering and load more posts without a full page reload datastar map explore locations with filters map markers and clustering '),(1623,'field',8,1,''),(1623,'slug',0,1,' temp iqpgdauuffllytjcwyogrxgxhycfvsvfeply '),