ve from day one twig is readable the structure is obvious no framework to learn first '),(2344,'slug',0,1,' temp uyaubxedqbwkzynnyccygcthkbdlxlprckdw '),(2344,'title',0,1,''),(2345,'field',4,1,' lower maintenance cost '),(2345,'field',8,1,' fewer moving parts means fewer things to break fewer dependencies to update and fewer midnight alerts '),(2345,'slug',0,1,' temp ywzunbwknwgwtnspsbfjsprndzucdpuxnwgt '),(2345,'title',0,1,''),(2346,'field',4,1,' one team one codebase '),(2346,'field',8,1,' no frontend backend split no handoff friction the whole project lives in one place owned by one team '),(2346,'slug',0,1,' temp xcorpmrrkzdbmjxejadaqrloumfxfbxsdnoa '),(2346,'title',0,1,''),(2347,'field',4,1,' ️ a platform with a roadmap '),(2347,'field',8,1,' craft 5 is lts five years of support after craft 6 ships craft 6 brings laravel under the hood the platform is going somewhere '),(2347,'slug',0,1,' temp lhnsndxhgzvscmicwaaqisxaawcxvaeccfez '),(2347,'title',0,1,''),(2348,'slug',0,1,''),(2409,'slug',0,1,' why craft kit '),(2409,'title',0,1,' why craft kit '),(2411,'field',8,1,' craft cms is becoming a laravel package here is what that means for you '),(2411,'field',10,1,' backend '),(2411,'field',17,1,''),(2411,'field',19,1,' craft 6 switches from yii 2 to laravel if you have never touched laravel this is the moment to pay attention at dot all 2025 in lisbon pixel tonic announced that craft cms 6 will be built on laravel the most widely used php framework in the world this is not a cosmetic update it is a fundamental shift in how craft works under the hood and it changes what you have access to as a developer if you have been building with craft for years and never needed laravel that is completely fine but understanding what this change brings and what it does not break is worth your time craft 6 is still in development beta is planned for q3 2026 general availability q4 2026 everything here is based on official announcements implementation details may evolve why laravel craft has been running on yii 2 since craft 3 yii 2 is a solid framework but it is a niche one the developer pool is small the ecosystem is limited and the tooling has not kept pace with what modern php development expects laravel is a different story entirely laravel has 70% market share among php developers over 25 000 composer packages a github community four times the size of yii 2 for craft developers that means a vastly larger ecosystem better tooling and a much bigger pool of developers who already know the underlying framework for craft developers this means access to a vastly larger ecosystem better tooling and if your agency hires a much larger pool of developers who already know the underlying framework craft is not abandoning what makes it craft it is adopting the infrastructure that lets it go further what you get access to once craft 6 runs on laravel the full laravel toolchain becomes available in your craft project not as third party integrations but as native capabilities you are not learning a new cms you are getting a much larger toolbox for the one you already use ⚙️ artisan cli all craft commands run via php artisan custom commands generators and task automation the way laravel developers already work ️ eloquent orm laravels database layer is available alongside crafts native element queries custom data models without fighting the framework 25 000 composer packages any laravel compatible package drops straight into your craft project payments notifications pdf generation already solved laravel octane in memory application server extreme performance gains without changing your application code laravel horizon professional queue monitoring scheduled publishing asset processing email all visible all manageable ️ laravel forge vapor deployment pipelines you may already be using craft now fits into them natively what happens to your existing projects craft 5 becomes an official lts release the moment craft 6 ships that means five full years of security updates and critical bug fixes no forced migration no deadline pressure for plugins pixel tonic is shipping a yii 2 adapter most existing craft plugins will run on craft 6 without code changes the upgrade path is designed to be gradual not a cliff craft 2 to craft 3 was painful pixel tonic learned from it this migration is built differently what is new for content editors the laravel migration is not the only story craft 6 also ships a set of long overdue editorial features that make the control panel meaningfully better for the people using it every day features your clients have been asking for since craft 3 they are finally first class ✅ content approval workflows built in approval processes for content publication custom rules precise control no plugin required commenting on entry edit pages editors can leave feedback directly on the editing screen no more back channel slack threads about a headline activity logs a full changelog for every content element who changed what and when content releases coordinate the publication of multiple entries at once campaign launches site relaunches handled the timeline q1 2026 laravel migration complete first alpha released q3 2026 beta approximately three months open testing q4 2026 general availability 2027 2031 craft 5 lts security and critical updates what to do now if you are on craft 5 stay there it is stable it is lts and there is no reason to rush use the time to get comfortable with laravel basics artisan eloquent the service container none of it is complicated if you already understand php and all of it will make you a more capable craft developer when craft 6 lands if you are starting a new project today craft 5 is still the right choice the architecture you build now clean templates proper element types structured content will carry forward cleanly the best time to learn laravel basics is before you need them that time is now read the official announcement '),(2411,'field',27,1,' craft cms 6 and laravel what craft developers need to know craft cms is moving towards laravel learn what the announced direction means for your projects which details need checking and why clean twig still matters '),(2411,'field',28,1,' craft cms is moving towards laravel learn what the announced direction means for your projects which details need checking and why clean twig still matters '),(2411,'field',35,1,' berlin germany '),(2411,'slug',0,1,' craft cms 6 laravel '),(2411,'title',0,1,' craft cms is becoming a laravel package '),(2412,'field',11,1,' craft cms is moving towards laravel learn what the announced direction means for your projects which details need checking and why clean twig still matters '),(2412,'slug',0,1,''),(2413,'field',4,1,' craft cms 6 and laravel what craft developers need to know craft cms is moving towards laravel learn what the announced direction means for your projects which details need checking and why clean twig still matters '),(2413,'slug',0,1,''),(2414,'field',20,1,' pixel tonic announced that craft cms 6 will move from yii 2 to laravel for craft developers the interesting question is what that foundation changes and what should remain familiar this article summarizes the announced direction not the current release status craft kit currently runs on craft cms 5 check the official laravel announcement and supported versions before planning a production upgrade why the framework matters the underlying framework supplies infrastructure for requests services commands and integrations laravel has an established ecosystem that craft can build on that creates opportunities but does not mean every laravel package or hosting tool automatically works with every craft project pixel tonic described the transition as mostly a port rather than a wholesale rewrite of crafts architecture the stated aim is to keep extending the cms familiar while changing the foundation underneath it what to evaluate the useful questions are concrete how custom modules boot which apis change which plugin versions support the release and what the chosen hosting environment requires test those against your own project rather than treating a framework name as a migration plan '),(2414,'slug',0,1,' temp dyzuagdpzcywlcsglsgmpeqdpnpfrabvemcr '),(2414,'title',0,1,''),(2415,'slug',0,1,''),(2416,'field',8,1,' what the framework change means '),(2416,'slug',0,1,' temp sopumlbdlehymcbywkjddpeesvdxwbpmyyfk '),(2416,'title',0,1,''),(2417,'slug',0,1,''),(2418,'field',4,1,' ⚙️ framework foundation '),(2418,'field',8,1,' laravel becomes the underlying framework in the announced craft 6 direction evaluate framework level changes against the actual release documentation '),(2418,'slug',0,1,' temp xbvlvqleuhqvydemtghsrjrebykqhzaowjoi '),(2418,'title',0,1,''),(2419,'field',4,1,' ️ plugin compatibility '),(2419,'field',8,1,' the announced yii 2 adapter aims to reduce migration work plugin support and custom module compatibility still need project specific testing '),(2419,'slug',0,1,' temp kaimyratqfzqrmtbxhxenksinmpblxjxuymk '),(2419,'title',0,1,''),(2420,'field',4,1,' a broader ecosystem '),(2420,'field',8,1,' laravel offers a large package ecosystem choose integrations for a real requirement and verify compatibility rather than assuming every package is a drop in '),(2420,'slug',0,1,' temp bbkovqgbxkjoobgnlbxannpponulgjkudqod '),(2420,'title',0,1,''),(2421,'field',4,1,' familiar content architecture '),(2421,'field',8,1,' the announcement emphasizes a port of the existing architecture structured content and deliberate template boundaries remain useful investments '),(2421,'slug',0,1,' temp zikohidxtyytfoxulbdnscurbazqhjrzmvau '),(2421,'title',0,1,''),(2422,'field',4,1,' a tested upgrade path '),(2422,'field',8,1,' rehearse upgrades on a local copy check project config and migrations and verify queues assets and custom code before touching production '),(2422,'slug',0,1,' temp dvircpqzyvmogztggiuadssoggqpyqhvhwfg '),(2422,'title',0,1,''),(2423,'field',4,1,' ️ current release guidance '),(2423,'field',8,1,' use official release notes and support dates for planning a roadmap describes an intention it is not confirmation that a feature has shipped '),(2423,'slug',0,1,' temp vnivgvksezxshwprmuwpolueoshfceqnoopx '),(2423,'title',0,1,''),(2424,'field',20,1,' what happens to existing projects the announcement includes a yii 2 adapter intended to reduce compatibility work for craft 5 plugins and modules that is an aid to migration not a guarantee that every custom integration will run unchanged check support from each plugin author and test the actual upgrade craft 5 is an lts release pixel tonic announced support for five years after craft 6s full release use the official support policy for current dates rather than calculating them from an old roadmap what editors can look forward to pixel tonic also announced editorial features for craft 6 treat the list below as announced plans check the release notes for what is available in a particular version '),(2424,'slug',0,1,' temp daocwrgiaptvxcfgnjafqyvxvvooqagobrms '),(2424,'title',0,1,''),(2425,'slug',0,1,''),(2426,'field',8,1,' announced editorial features '),(2426,'slug',0,1,' temp lqwrfrtjyioxrehlxfhluumivnrvpkgqfrcw '),(2426,'title',0,1,''),(2427,'slug',0,1,''),(2428,'field',4,1,' ✅ approval workflows '),(2428,'field',8,1,' announced governance features would let teams define how content moves through review and publication verify availability in the version you choose '),(2428,'slug',0,1,' temp yypbfzsagpbfklflctpkpchweyezdiuowapm '),(2428,'title',0,1,''),(2429,'field',4,1,' edit page comments '),(2429,'field',8,1,' feedback directly on an entry edit page is part of the announced editorial direction keeping discussion close to the content it concerns '),(2429,'slug',0,1,' temp fevpxsvfnavmrzqqfuklzbelfudcsxtmrqsx '),(2429,'title',0,1,''),(2430,'field',4,1,' element activity logs '),(2430,'field',8,1,' the announced activity timeline is intended to make changes to an element easier to follow including who changed what and when '),(2430,'slug',0,1,' temp biwjmbvbgpgialmddcccczhnqnjjmrediczs '),(2430,'title',0,1,''),(2431,'field',4,1,' content releases '),(2431,'field',8,1,' publishing related entries and drafts together manually or on a schedule is one of the announced improvements for coordinated launches '),(2431,'slug',0,1,' temp ypuyxwhfugyjxcjizashheeojuvutjwnnfue '),(2431,'title',0,1,''),(2432,'field',20,1,' roadmaps are not release guarantees the original announcement targeted a 2026 beta and full release schedules and implementation details can change confirm the current release migration guidance and plugin compatibility directly with craft before setting a client deadline what this means for craft kit and datastar the architectural reason for datastar does not depend on laravel craft renders html and datastar coordinates reactive browser state and server driven updates a backend framework change is not a reason to introduce a separate frontend application craft kit demonstrates that approach today on craft 5 its navigation dropdowns search panel blog filters and todo list use datastar rather than alpine or another general purpose frontend framework try the blog search and todo list or inspect the starter kit on github build a clean content model and well defined template boundaries now assess any future framework migration against the actual released apis '),(2432,'slug',0,1,' temp uflbgcwhxputwgsgexaegqeotkzofmghxvrb '),(2432,'title',0,1,''),(2433,'slug',0,1,''),(2434,'alt',0,1,''),(2434,'extension',0,1,' jpg '),(2434,'filename',0,1,' markus winkler qpjv8xaxptq unsplash jpg '),(2434,'kind',0,1,' image '),(2434,'slug',0,1,''),(2434,'title',0,1,' markus winkler q pj v8 xa xptq unsplash '),(2435,'field',8,1,' read the official announcement '),(2435,'field',11,1,''),(2435,'slug',0,1,' temp xaygllieqyndoyvhtocnpvadjmpxecbkomtm '),(2435,'title',0,1,''),(2436,'slug',0,1,''),(2491,'field',20,1,' the illustrations were created for craft kit with an image generation model ai generated assets are marked by the shared image component '),(2491,'slug',0,1,''),(2491,'title',0,1,''),(2525,'alt',0,1,' a tactile architectural model of orange ivory and graphite blocks arranged as a modular page '),(2525,'extension',0,1,' png '),(2525,'field',4,1,' reusable blocks arranged as a page ai generated illustration for craft kit '),(2525,'filename',0,1,' builder matrix blocks png '),(2525,'kind',0,1,' image '),(2525,'slug',0,1,''),(2525,'title',0,1,' matrix modular page blocks '),(2526,'alt',0,1,' an open ivory folio with paragraph like grooves and inset orange and graphite components '),(2526,'extension',0,1,' png '),(2526,'field',4,1,' components embedded in the flow of a story ai generated illustration for craft kit '),(2526,'filename',0,1,' builder ckeditor flow png '),(2526,'kind',0,1,' image '),(2526,'slug',0,1,''),(2526,'title',0,1,' ckeditor components in the flow '),(2527,'alt',0,1,' four upright ivory orange and graphite card like blocks arranged on a low plinth '),(2527,'extension',0,1,' png '),(2527,'field',4,1,' different pieces one consistent system ai generated illustration for craft kit '),(2527,'filename',0,1,' builder reusable cards png '),(2527,'kind',0,1,' image '),(2527,'slug',0,1,''),(2527,'title',0,1,' reusable content cards '),(2528,'alt',0,1,' a folded ivory accordion sculpture with an orange central panel and graphite end supports '),(2528,'extension',0,1,' png '),(2528,'field',4,1,' a structure that opens when you need more detail ai generated illustration for craft kit '),(2528,'filename',0,1,' builder accordion folds png '),(2528,'kind',0,1,' image '),(2528,'slug',0,1,''),(2528,'title',0,1,' expandable accordion '),(2533,'field',20,1,' two ways to compose content the same reusable twig components build pages from separate matrix blocks or embed those components in the flow of a ckeditor field this demo shows both approaches side by side explore matrix explore ckeditor matrix build the page each block is an entry with its own fields and twig template add arrange and edit blocks in craft a shared content container keeps the page aligned while each wrapper controls backgrounds and spacing '),(2533,'slug',0,1,''),(2533,'title',0,1,''),(2534,'slug',0,1,''),(2535,'slug',0,1,''),(2535,'title',0,1,''),(2536,'slug',0,1,''),(2537,'field',12,1,' how does the matrix builder work a matrix field holds a sequence of entries with different types here those types represent page blocks editors choose a block fill in its fields and move it into place twig renders each entry through its block template text use a text block for headings paragraphs lists and links this introduction is a text block rich text stays editable in craft while its presentation comes from the shared typography styles media a media block displays an image video or audio asset with an optional caption the image example below uses responsive image transforms and includes a photographer credit view the media block buttons group links to entries or external urls in a button block the example links to other demos in this starter kit view the button block cards each card combines a headline and teaser with an optional image and link add and reorder the nested card entries inside the block view the card block accordions you are using an accordion block now each item has a heading rich text and an initial open or closed state use it for faqs or supporting details that readers can expand when needed gallery select and arrange several images in one block the page shows a preview grid opening an image launches the lightbox with the full selection view the gallery block slider the slider presents nested card entries in an embla carousel use the arrow buttons or drag the cards to browse this example uses cards without images view the slider block call to action a call to action block combines a headline optional text and a link use it to give a section a clear next step view the call to action block '),(2537,'slug'